select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "46d73e8c-07aa-4f98-9379-eb19a12270e5", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (08:05): Yeah, that's interesting. You guys are entirely D two CI feel like especially with the explosion of bike sales in the last eight months or so, you guys have probably had a lot of new, just people who are new to the bike world get interested in bikes. Has that been difficult for them as they've been because they don't have the benefit of being able to go to a store, help get sized help find the right bike? How have you guys tried to help them? Someone who doesn't know anything about the bike get into the bike entirely from the computer?", "token_estimate": 141, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:05", "end_timestamp": "08:05", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "28376097-2aff-4fe7-b3d2-74f3b9c876f1", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Kyle McCann (08:38): Totally, yeah. It's kind of a two-pronged approach that we've taken. So number one, our sales staff is incredible. We have direct service internal, so I want to say we're at 15 customer service agents and you can reach them from 7:00 AM to 4:00 PM Pacific time, and those folks are just on the ball, know every bike and can walk through sizing the right bike for the right ride style, so on and so forth. And these folks are, like I mentioned, just dialed when it comes to knowing the bikes and helping customers through the journey. Number two, I wouldn't necessarily say we've launched something called service marketing, but we've focused more on something we're calling service marketing. So what we're looking at is frequently asked questions, what is customer service hearing all the time? So like you mentioned, there's new customers, they have tons of questions, they've never ridden a bike and we don't blame it on them. (09:43): We're trying to get as many people on bikes as possible by offering the best bike at the best price. So it brings in a diverse set of people. So what we've done on the website, we've really made it so that there's frequently asked questions in the right place at the right time. So if you make it to a product detail page and we think that you're going to have a question about that specific bike, there's a likely chance you'll run into it when you need it. So we've really merchandised the website to answer questions. And then another thing we've done under that service marketing umbrella is within our newsletter cadence, within our social cadence and our just outbound communication cadence where answering those frequently asked questions just generically. So we'll post a social posts today, we're going to answer your FAQs about assembly. How easy is it to assemble a bike? Can I bring to my local bike shop? Yes. Can I send it to Velo Fix? Yes. Can I have them repair it for me? The answer is yes to all of those things. So we've been rolling out for the past couple of months this campaign where we've dubbed why are they calling? So basically just getting to know our customers better and then actually answering their questions and using their feedback has been, yeah, it's really been helpful.", "token_estimate": 567, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann"], "start_timestamp": "08:38", "end_timestamp": "08:38", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "6fe49130-fc8b-44d1-b042-a891fca1477c", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:13): And what a great opportunity too. Those are all potential lifelong customers, so it goes well, hopefully, like you said, Philip their garage.\n\nKyle McCann (11:22): Yeah, exactly. It's funny, I've always heard this thing since I've been in marketing. Your actual customer that you have is five x more likely to convert than any other customer, and it's totally true. You have to use what you have or the people that are loyal to you to market your bikes. I mean, gorilla Marketing 1 0 1 or Referral Marketing 1 0 1, your customer is your biggest advocate, and if you make them happy in the beginning and you make it seem like, or you make it known that you are the leader in their bike experience, there'll be a customer forever. And that's something we're really leaning into now.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:02): So to change gears a little bit, how did you end up at Canyon? I know you had a bit of a winding road to get here.", "token_estimate": 236, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kyle McCann"], "start_timestamp": "11:13", "end_timestamp": "12:02", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "dd9cadb3-57c2-4741-a61e-685a6fb664bc", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Kyle McCann (12:12): We talked about it before I called it a jumping ship, and I've literally done exactly that. So prior to Canyon, I was at the Enthusiast Network, so most familiar in outdoor industry, surfer magazine, all of the transworld publications, powder Bike Mag, and I was the group digital marketing manager there. So really got hands-on with the brands, but not just the brands, the advertisers as well, and help them with the strategy on how they were going to deploy their campaigns with the brands and then how they would measure the campaigns, and then actually pulling the levers. So if let's say a company wants to spend with Bike Mag and they want their article that we wrote for them or their video that we produce for them amplified on social, I would choose the audience and then pull the levers within Facebook or Instagram or YouTube. However, we decided to push that and really got my hands dirty there and learned a lot about the outdoor industry, just being that the Enthusiast Network as a conglomerate or a collaborative has every action sport that I can think of. (13:33): Even for a while there was sort of freelancing under one of the brands, so I was doing editorial for Kayak Fish Mag for about six months of my time there, and it just shows the diversity of the brands that were there. But unfortunately as we're talking now, as we know, some of those brands have kind of gone under the gun for a bit now, so unfortunate to see. But while I was there, they went through a buyout with American Media Inc. Also the owners of Men's Journal, and they did a huge layoff morale there. It's hard. Layoffs are really hard, and I made it through that and a few months later, canyon reached out to me and they were actually a mile down the road from our office, so it was a no-brainer for me. I've been mountain biking for a pretty long time and knew the digital marketing space well. (14:40): So it was easy conversion and that's actually, so 10, the Enthusiast Network brought me down to San Diego from San Francisco. I was there for, it was like three and a half years before that 10 took me from Twitter. I was an account manager there. So helping small to medium sized businesses utilize their ad spend or optimize their ad spend on the Twitter ads platform. I spent about six months there under the hood really learning their ads platform, which surprisingly, or maybe not surprisingly to those in the ad world is very similar to any of the other ad platforms, but a different audience with a different mindset really looking for news and updates. So it was really cool to learn a platform that I hadn't spent a ton of time on and figure out what these advertisers were using it for, because everyone's spending their money on Facebook, everyone's spending their money on Google AdWords, why are people spending money on Twitter? And it was a totally different reason. And that was what was the", "token_estimate": 732, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann"], "start_timestamp": "12:12", "end_timestamp": "12:12", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "45b7ca26-edc3-480f-9dc3-044750e01af0", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:57): Reason.\n\nKyle McCann (15:59): It was like I said, people were looking for news and updates and that was what advertisers were providing in their paid ad spend. They were really looking to provide updates on their brands. So I'd say one of the biggest differences was that the brands would retarget their followers on Twitter, and this is what I saw the most of, to basically highlight their updates. So product updates, new products just in stock now kind of vibe to it. So it seemed really different in the way that I think of Facebook personally, where I'm thinking of it as like I'm trying to get really prospect, so I'm looking for anyone that's potentially interested in Viking, whereas Twitter, it didn't seem like a lot of the brands were utilizing it as such. And so that was my time at Twitter. Prior to that I was at Facebook, so under the hood, they're analyzing pretty large sets of data to uncover any issues in the search algorithm. (17:09): So basically if you type in, let's say the team that I worked on was people, places and things. If you typed in someone's name and it brought up an incorrect query like you typed in Coleborn and it brought up Kyle McCann obviously an issue. So I would work with our product managers and engineers to provide them the data that a large team of basically they call them Raiders and labelers. So you would rate the data and then you'd provide these data sets back to the product managers and engineers to find out what the heck's going on, why is this wrong? And you'd uncover or they would uncover clear issues, but it was awesome experience there. That was probably as a workplace, the coolest place I've ever worked at. I lived in SF in the city proper and there down in Menlo Park, and it takes around 45 minutes or so to get down there. (18:12): And they had these basically just charter buses with wifi and TVs on them, and you'd work for the first 45 minutes of the day via the bus or you could take a nap, whatever you wanted. And then you get there and there's three little mess halls to choose from for breakfast, and then there's eight or 10 restaurants for lunch, and then same thing for dinner. So they did a good job at wanting me to stay there and not leave. I even took woodworking classes there. They had a full size, it's probably 3000 square feet, square foot wood shop. I built, I want to say six or eight fly fishing nets. While I was there just on my lunch break, I would walk a hundred feet from my desk and there's this, I mean, all access wood shop that you could use. It was incredible. I mean, just seeing under the hood there was insane. (19:13): And then prior to that, I worked for a direct consumer paddleboard company. They had just launched when they brought me on board. And I had actually never done any marketing sales job with the title. I'd never been a sales or marketing manager before that. And it was a friend of a friend and I had gotten pretty decent at taking photos or so I thought, and he was like, Hey, would you like to come take some photos of these boards that I just got? I'll pay you to do it. Sure, yeah, of course, man. And one thing led to another, and about a year and a half later I was managing the marketing and sales for the entirety of the company. And again, that was a direct consumer company. So that was really where I got my chops on learning the ins and outs of the paid programs or performance marketing and then the content marketing and how it all really meshes together in a direct consumer brand. (20:17): And then to my first topic, the jumping ship for about four years, three, four years after high school, I worked on private motor yachts and sport fishing boats. And one of the boats took me across from the Caribbean to Mexico and from Mexico to San Francisco. And when I hit San Francisco, I saw the opportunity there as just a means an end for me in the sport fishing and yachting community. And I literally jumped ship. I saw I had an opportunity that I took with the paddleboard company and kind of just ran with it from there. And that was my start into the industry.", "token_estimate": 1032, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kyle McCann"], "start_timestamp": "15:57", "end_timestamp": "15:59", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "b231aa28-effb-4ba5-9bed-5d91f51f3091", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (21:03): When you were on the boat, did you know that your end goal was to get off and then find some job in San Francisco, or did this opportunity just kind of pop up and you're like, this sounds cool?\n\nKyle McCann (21:14): Yeah, it was kind of a mix of both. I was like, I can't do this forever. I don't know how many people watch Below Deck, but it's a funny show on Bravo where they kind of make fun of the yachties and people working on boats. And as you can see from the show, you don't have a big life outside of the boat. And I knew that eventually I wanted a life, so maybe it didn't have to happen in San Francisco, but it did because of that opportunity that I had. And that's kind of one of the things that I've learned over the years is I don't necessarily believe in luck. I just believe in taking the opportunities that you see. And that's been really helpful. And that was definitely what I took there was I had an opportunity, I knew that there was a good potential that San Francisco was a great place for me to take off my career or change my career path right place at\n\nCole Heilborn (22:12): Right time for sure. But marketing, it wasn't something you were interested in, it just\n\nKyle McCann (22:19): Happened. It's kind of funny you say that. I haven't really thought of it before that I was super interested in photography and then as a kid, I've always considered myself an entrepreneur. When I was eight, I used to, between eight and 10, I used to empty our garage once a month or so and go through the things that my mom didn't want, and I would create these garage sale signs that I would put in front of our neighborhood, and I would have my own garage sales from the\n\nCole Heilborn (22:53): Trash your mom threw out from", "token_estimate": 441, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kyle McCann"], "start_timestamp": "21:03", "end_timestamp": "22:53", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "4b22ef0a-cbc2-4db4-b06a-4c529f0d9b0c", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Kyle McCann (22:55): The crap my mom threw out. And I kind of go back to that always. And there's other stories that I can remember. I built a trailer for my lawn mower to put on the back of my bike as a kid and beat on doors in the neighborhood and mowed people's lawns. It's just been always like I'd consider myself more of an entrepreneur and aware of many hats versus a digital marketer. And that's, I feel like I'm growing into that more growth marketing position as I grow in my career.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:33): Okay, interesting. So some would say you're at Facebook, you've got free wifi, free transportation, you've got a shop, you got free meals, why leave?\n\nKyle McCann (23:45): Dude, I ask myself that question all the time. It's really interesting to see how Facebook handles certain things these days. And maybe I didn't think about it then that I was okay with their handlings of, let's say in particular privacy or data or freedom of speech. It's a huge company. They handle so many things and we're talking today on election day. It's insane the amount of things that both left and right are scared of with these social media platforms. And previous conversation we talked about that new movie Social Dilemma, a lot of that crap is real. They are, I wouldn't say in our heads yet, but they can darn near predict what we're going to do next. It's relatively scary, and I don't know if in my journey of life that at this point I would want to be under the hood there, although I respect the company very much and I think that it's amazing what they've been able to do and the technology that they've built is incredible. The people behind that company are just wizard smart, just incredible. But it might step a little over the things that I believe in maybe at this point. So maybe when I was there, I wasn't thinking about it as much because they're really good at making you feel good, like you said,\n\nCole Heilborn (25:24): Because you were making fly fishing nets on your lunch breaks.", "token_estimate": 502, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:55", "end_timestamp": "25:24", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "f6805ed8-3839-43a6-a4bd-45632482ccde", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Kyle McCann (25:30): It's pretty awesome. I mean, there's a football field in a football league, in a basketball league. I didn't want to leave the office ever in the moment. It was awesome. But now maybe I'd reconsider if I had the opportunity, I might go back, but I definitely reconsider a lot more these days.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:51): Yeah, no, it's interesting you brought up the social dilemma because actually about two hours ago I was recording another episode that is coming out. It'll have come out by the time your episode comes out. It's with Mary Kate, she was the senior marketing strategist at Goal Zero, and we just talked about the social dilemma and the things that, she's watched it, she's been asking herself after watching it for her job. She's constantly managing social platforms. And so not to go too far down into that topic, but if folks are interested, you could listen to that episode. It's the one right after. But back to your story, I'm curious though, maybe you didn't realize what you realize now about Twitter or Facebook, but you still ended up leaving what Motivated Leave?\n\nKyle McCann (26:44): Yeah, so while I was at Facebook, I was like I mentioned under the hood, looking at data, not really connecting with people. And the opportunity that I had at Twitter was actually connecting with people and helping their brands. And I've found in recent, or at least since working at the Enthusiast Network, probably that my mission or my goal is to really connect brands with their target customer. And that is what excites me and gets me stoked is figuring out what makes a potential customer tick and how to have that path of least resistance. And I've gone at that full bore since leaving Facebook and trying to figure out which pieces of the digital marketing architecture touch people at the right point to get them there to that conversion and then to that brand's identity or equity that all brands are searching for. So I think it was a mix of not being connected with people and then just opportunity for sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:01): What about connecting brands with consumers is your personal motivation, why is that exciting?", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:30", "end_timestamp": "28:01", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "b61958e0-9f92-410e-adf0-00f35f7a71d1", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Kyle McCann (28:10): That's a good question. I'm looking at it from my position now. The mission for Canyon as inspiring riders to Ride is one that I can really identify with. I personally, my greatest passion is being outdoors. If I could make a living off of just being outdoors, I don't know what that would be, maybe an influencer or something. Don't tell my girlfriend I said that. But I just love the outdoors and brands that connect people with the outdoors or connect people with being happy outside like Canyon does. I have a strong passion for riding mountain bikes, and Canyon gives me that ability to go ride on mountains that I potentially couldn't have from another brand. I mean, sure I can buy any bike, but to me, canyon is the best of any bike brand out there. And it's been awesome connecting so many people with bikes, especially with the pandemic going on really wild to see not just mountain biking, but also how biking in general is changing. (29:27): Like I said earlier, turning into a means of transportation. I'm personally a little bit, or I'm hyper aware of COVID, I try not to in huge public places with no outdoor seating, try to sit outside if I'm going somewhere and let's say if I was in San Francisco still, I didn't have a car there, I wouldn't have wanted to ride a bus and a bike would've been the next best thing. And we're giving that opportunity to a ton of people. So I think really my passion is connecting people with the outdoors and if it's through brands that do that for them even better. And especially brands that protect the outdoors space that they're focused on. So trail advocacy for us is a big thing. And then working with athletes that also help people get outdoors is super helpful to make me happy and what I'm doing.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:29): Did you know that was a big motivator, a big thing that you needed in your life? Or is this something you've kind of discovered after you've started working at the Enthusiast Network and at Canyon? Both outdoor brands?", "token_estimate": 505, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "28:10", "end_timestamp": "30:29", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "6c98e5e1-d94d-4bcc-a4d4-4ec3b382f562", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Kyle McCann (30:41): I think that actually is something that I found out in working at 10 and Canyon, the Outdoors has been a lifelong passion for me as a kid. My grandfather was a fishing rod builder, my dad was a firefighter and a fly fishing guide. I've always been connected with fishing and the outdoors in the conservation thereof because if we don't really focus on it, it's not going to be there for future generations. And working with actual brands that advocate for the outdoors or advocate for those specific places has been an eyeopener. And working at both of those places has drawn my vision to that exact point where I'm at now.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:29): Maybe for the outdoor industry specifically, do you think it's important for marketers to be super niche focused into one element? Or was it more useful to be kind of a broad good at a lot of different things versus an expert at one? And not that you're not an expert, but you see where I'm going with that?", "token_estimate": 244, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:41", "end_timestamp": "31:29", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "a6835b5b-b83d-4a81-8863-81d9ab3550cd", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Kyle McCann (31:50): Yeah, totally. And I think that brings up a good point. Working at these larger companies and even looking at the larger group of Canyon, they have their teams somewhat siloed and Canyon us, we are pretty lean marketing team, especially the digital marketing team. It's really, if we want to say performance marketing and marketing, automation is all on me. So when it comes to collaboration with the entirety of the team, we're really special in that we speak with one another every day. Even through this kind of time of not being together, I get with my brand marketing manager every day, we're on the horn all the time. Same with the web manager, same thing goes for our events manager. She's kind of gotten into a different role now and we are all on a constant conversing with each other. And that's not just marketing. I have to talk with it, talk with customer service, and going back to our service marketing that brought in customer service into our daily workflow, I would say that having a smaller team and a wider skillset to me has been so much more helpful in that I can bring everyone's expertise together and be kind of a gatekeeper for digital marketing, but have all of these different perspectives touching all the pieces of it. (33:36): I would recommend to learn as much as possible about as many things as possible. In my perspective. I wouldn't hone in on one specific thing, but that's just me.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:47): Well, and I suppose the beauty of being a generalist is that you can then see everything and then you can decide when you need a specialist for whatever role you're trying to fill, because you kind of understand the whole picture.\n\nKyle McCann (34:02): And maybe I'm setting myself up to be more of a manager. I get. Yeah, it is definitely something that I pride myself in knowing a lot about a lot.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:14): One of my last questions, and it is a two part question. How has your career shaped who you are and how has your personal life shaped your career and how have they overlapped or worked together or not always work together?", "token_estimate": 528, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:50", "end_timestamp": "34:14", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "1b1c4741-c341-4582-8f2f-b13c22b7f6f0", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Kyle McCann (34:31): Yeah, I, like I mentioned, started out as a fisherman as a kid, and the conservation there of the places that I fished were super specific. I grew up in down in the Florida Keys and Fish, the Everglades, since I was knee high to a grasshopper and have been fishing that place my entire life. So it means a hell of a lot to me, a lot, a lot. And getting exposed to all of these different sports. The one topic that stays on my mind all the time is the conservation of those places that you do those sports in. So now I'm a mountain biker. I wasn't a mountain biker forever. Now I surf. Well, now I'm in Richmond, Virginia. I don't really surf, but now I float on the river. I mean paddle, do all kinds of sports, and there's very specific tactics that you have to take to conserve the places that, those things that happen where you recreate. So I think as a human being, I've learned to think about more of the places and the things that matter most to me and help advocate for them, the conservation of those places. I don't know if that answered your question. I feel like I went off kind of a tangent, but\n\nCole Heilborn (36:01): No, that's good. So it kind of sounds like your guiding light has been how could I use my career or the things that I've learned in my career to help preserve the places that I love\n\nKyle McCann (36:10): Fully. And I think that's your first part of the question. Maybe\n\nCole Heilborn (36:17): I didn't have an objective with the question other than just curious to see what would come out. Kyle, we're about out of time for today, but thanks for spending the last hour, 50 minutes or so, not an hour and 50 an hour chatting. It's cool to get a sense for where your career has gone and maybe excited to see where it's going to go.\n\nKyle McCann (36:38): Yeah. Thanks for having me on, Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:40): Alright, take care. You too. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 526, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "34:31", "end_timestamp": "36:40", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "aafe6f01-204c-4e52-85ae-58caf9309708", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Eli Kerr (00:02): One of the differentiators for us at Gnarly is that because I have this pretty unique background, that all of that stuff has come with me and it's become part of what we are about at Gnarly and it's part of our brand. This idea of progression and helping people become better, and it's not about being the best, it's about being your best.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer, I hope you enjoy today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hoborne. Today I'm sitting down with Eli Kerr, the CO slash co-founder of Gnarly Nutrition. Eli, how are you?\n\nEli Kerr (01:16): Oh, fantastic. Pretty good morning. Feeling a lot, but really excited to be with you, Cole, and have a conversation today.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:25): Your unofficial, maybe official nickname is the Naza. I'm curious where this name came from because I think, well, let's just start there.\n\nEli Kerr (01:35): Well, so the idea of a czar is a person that's in control or managing, I mean, I guess I don't know the technical definition, but is in charge of whatever responsibility that Czar is over, and I happen to be over gnarly. So the idea is the czar of nar. So really trying to bring gnarly more out of the depths and encourage all people to be more gnarly.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:09): Well, I think when I was introduced to you, one of the first things that was said was, oh, and his nickname is the Naza, and I was like, well, man, this guy sounds like a cool guy to meet. So super excited to chat today. I would love to hear a little bit more on the background of Gnarly and what you guys do.", "token_estimate": 535, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "02:09", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "558f8bec-d0b9-4c71-bdce-44ccadbaa0a3", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Eli Kerr (02:26): Yeah, so sometimes I can go off, so if you ever need to throw the lasso and bring me in, please, I'll lasso it. It's funny, actually, I consider myself a relatively quiet guy, but then when I start talking about gnarly and stuff like that, it seems like I just start rattling and going off. And anyways, so the lasso comes or the sheep hook comes in hand here. So we started about eight years ago really focused on, and the idea behind gnarly was to create quality natural sports nutrition products that tasted delicious. So we felt like there was this place in this very saturated market where there was actually a hole where natural products didn't taste very good. The other products that weren't natural did taste really good, but nobody was putting the two together and really making good quality products that were delicious that people could look forward to using. (03:34): And that's really where Gnarly was born. And then we saw this other hole in the outdoor industry where we felt like we could also feel we had a background in the outdoor industry and knew it and felt like we could bring products that were traditionally known in more of the fitness industry and less known in the outdoor industry, and bring them over and educate outdoor athletes on how to use products like these and thus fill this hole around performance and recovery for outdoor athletes. And so that's kind of the journey that we started on is really bringing performance sports, nutrition products to outdoor athletes. And it's been a hell of a ride. It's been really interesting. There's been a lot of barriers that we haven't or didn't see initially, but we've also been really welcomed. And I think that's partly because we've taken a pretty authentic approach. We've also been really focused on the science of products like ours and less just on straight marketing and sales of products like this. And then also we've had the opportunity and been, I guess blessed with a lot of phenomenal relationships with athletes that are outstanding in the mountain culture that have come to love our products and fully adopt them. So that's been super rad and a really fulfilling part of what we do. (05:21): Ultimately, we have wanted to create a lifestyle brand that supports outdoor athletes and helps them become better and do more of what they love. So our slogan is want more, do more, be more. It's this idea of a process that we all go through to become something greater than what we might be today or were yesterday and our products help a little bit with that process is what we believe we're here to support. We also want to ultimately support the athlete through more holistic approaches as well, and not just products. And I think that that's really where we are trying to go as a brand. And that's what I mean by a lifestyle brand is people who use our products and people who are associated with us see us much more than just a product-based company.", "token_estimate": 741, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "02:26", "end_timestamp": "02:26", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "13525f6c-189e-4bd9-aa78-ca128a1ae93c", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:22): To clarify, you guys make powders, you guys make, what type of nutritional products do you supply?\n\nEli Kerr (06:28): Cool. Yeah, we primarily actually only make based products.\n\nSpeaker 3 (06:35): So\n\nEli Kerr (06:36): Everything we do is powdered and everything is consumable product. We don't do any food-based products right now, meaning like bars or anything like that, but everything traditionally we would put in the category of training products. So these are products such as everything from a pre-workout that you would take to help your performance during an event or during a training session. And then branch chain amino acids that help with recovery all the way to proteins, which have kind of been our traditional flagship product, both a grassed, whe, and a vegan product that you use for both help with macros of protein, but also post-workout or event to help with recovery. We also have a hydration mix. We also have a creatine product. All of our products are really focused on helping you with performance during training and ultimately prepare for an event, a game day, and then with recovery.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:55): Gotcha, gotcha. So you said eight years ago you guys started because you kind of saw a need to blend healthy nutrition products with tasting nutrition, but then as you kept going you realized maybe there was this other opportunity where you could dive into the outdoor industry and supply your products there. Is that a gap? Is there a gap in that market? I mean, obviously sports nutrition is a big thing, but is nutrition for the outdoor industry as popular or as well known?", "token_estimate": 410, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "06:22", "end_timestamp": "07:55", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "972a2b6b-bc7d-4119-bbac-70f57224a6a3", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Eli Kerr (08:27): Well, so the two events happened simultaneously for, (08:32): It wasn't necessarily like an order of operations. It really happened simultaneously. And what we also saw during that same time is that functional fitness was growing substantially and functional fitness was also really colliding with the outdoor industry. And so for the, I would say not necessarily for the first time, but it became more generally accepted and or part of the outdoor industry for outdoor athletes to start moving more towards the gym, meaning training specifically for their sports. When I grew up, we just did more of our sport to get us better and stronger at our sport. Now the environment for an outdoor athlete has really changed, and a lot of outdoor athletes are using training programs, training at a traditional gym setting using trainers. (09:36): It's totally different. And that created a really, really cool space where we could come in with sports nutrition products, which there was a gap, and provide sports nutrition products specifically around training. And so what we've been working on is helping people understand the science of products like these, because traditionally they see products like these and they look at 'em and they're like, oh, that's for the gym rat, that's for the muscle builders, that's for the dude or gal that's getting super yoked. And that's just not true. That's not the reality of it. The truth is that these products actually help with your performance and help with recovery so that you can continue to progress and do more of the sport you like. They don't just necessarily make you pack on muscle. So there was definitely a gap in the outdoor industry around the knowledge of products like ours, and there still is and an acceptance of these kinds of products for the outdoor athlete. There are outdoor athletes who have been doing more traditional training forever, but there were few, and now it's become more accepted. Same thing with products like ours. There have been outdoor athletes that have been using products like ours forever, but now it's becoming more accepted and we've definitely been pushing and creating that desire or that acceptance of products like ours. So there's been traditionally products that are more carbohydrate based that outdoor athletes have been using, and these are more fuel based products that you would recognize whether there were bars or chews or a gel (11:32): That you use as a fuel substance. And we don't currently provide products like that. And that space is also very saturated in the outdoor industry. So everybody from Cliff to bar to go, those are the brands that we as being involved in the outdoor space and have been for quite some time.", "token_estimate": 686, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "08:27", "end_timestamp": "08:27", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "083d211a-57df-497a-b1fc-facd8dd06118", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:59): Gotcha. Why do you feel like the outdoor industry, has the athletes have lagged in terms of nutritional knowledge when compared to a counterpart in the sports workout in, I dunno, industry or side of it?\n\nEli Kerr (12:21): That's a pretty interesting question. I mean, I guess we could theorize, right?\n\nCole Heilborn (12:29): Yeah. I don't know. Do you have any ideas?\n\nEli Kerr (12:32): I think that partly it's because, and this is totally theoretical, but watching some other sports nutrition brands try to come into the outdoor space now because it's growing and it's becoming bigger. Before the outdoor space has really been sports, whether we call 'em extreme sports or fringe sports, they're the sports that are on the outside of mainstream sports, less people from a mass or from a scaling perspective. It's been a relatively small group. In some ways it is, if you're in the outdoor industry, you think that the outdoor industry is huge and it is, but it's also really relatively small. And from the number of people that participate relative to mass sports, it's really small. So for larger companies who have traditionally sold or made these products, I think that one reason is that there's not a huge ROI for going into that industry.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:49): Interesting.\n\nEli Kerr (13:50): And I think that now what we're seeing is as the outdoor industry continues to grow at an alarming rate, that it's becoming more interesting for other brands and we've seen and continue to see more sports nutrition brands now wanting and trying to enter the outdoor market.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:11): Yeah, I mean I feel like semi-recently, what five, 10 years that doing the outdoor thing has become kind of cool and yeah, maybe that seems like a good theory. Well, we can revisit that in a year and see if that still stands true. So let's shift gears a little bit. I'm curious to learn a little bit more about your background. Gnarly's only been around for eight years or so. What were you doing prior?", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "11:59", "end_timestamp": "14:11", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "c4ecade8-146e-462e-857d-59f077e2f2a2", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Eli Kerr (14:44): It feels like a lifetime ago that I was doing something other than gnarly. Yeah. My background originally was in a field called recreation therapy, so it's really mental health. I went to college and got a recreation therapy degree here. I'm based in Utah and Salt Lake City in Utah, it's a certified position. And so basically recreation therapy, you use recreation as a modality to help people and it serves a lot of different kinds of people, like large demographics as far as it can be community-based programs or it can be more mental health. I specifically focused on more mental health and even more specifically focused a lot with adolescents. So working with kids typically from 12 up to 18 years old started out in what most people are probably familiar or have heard of wilderness type programs. So started there and moved through that into more residential programs and then also really focused on more adventure based programs, so using not only recreation but specifically adventure type recreation. (16:14): So the stuff that we love in the outdoors, so whether it was skiing programs, climbing programs, I mean backpacking, canyoneering, we used all these different modalities to help and build these adolescents up and used it as a form of therapy. And so I spent a lot of years doing that. I did a business minor in college as well, so I always had this inkling and desire to be more involved in business, and it was always very exciting for me to want to try to do something and then towards, as I was, I guess now I can say wrapping up my career in recreation therapy, I had a desire to actually go practice medicine in some way. So I went back to school and did a bunch of prerequisites so that I could actually go to PA school, and I had just completed all those and was applying to PA schools when the opportunity for gnarly came about and got whisked away. And I can't say that I've never looked back. So I've definitely looked back, especially during some of the hard times of trying to grow and build gnarly, there's these times it's like, oh, I would love to just have this consistent job where as a pa I can live wherever I want. I get paid well, I practice doing really rad stuff with humans, but I didn't go in that route. And so (18:16): That's kind of the background.", "token_estimate": 580, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "14:44", "end_timestamp": "14:44", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "000a2807-0f4f-4437-b24e-78bb1a6f8a26", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:18): Nice. So I'm curious because a lot of people, it seems like a quick antidote to a lot of people is like, oh, just get outside, go to a park, go on a run, you will feel better. I'm curious from your perspective as someone who's done this professionally, are there any success stories or any examples that you can talk about for things that you saw how people improved as they spent more time outside or gained confidence and skills outside?\n\nEli Kerr (18:53): Good question. So one thing I guess I could say is I would talk about this subject from more of a general sense, (19:05): And I do believe it's true everything that you just mentioned, I think it's totally true, and I think that we have a really, really good case study of that in the year 2020 where shit has hit the fan and has completely and totally disrupted our lives and what we expected our year to be like. And what's really been powerful to see, and for some people who love the outdoors and who were already in there, I think are frustrated or maybe even bothered or upset because there has been this calling of people just crowds heading outside because for a lot of reasons are normal forms of entertainment have been removed. And so people looking for things to do and they've headed outdoors. It's been a beautiful thing. I think that why I say it's a case study is that we have seen how people have found solace there. (20:16): We have seen how people have found some form of stability and connection back to our earth through that and connection back to humans, which is so wild that during all this COVID in 2020 that we're trying to create some social distancing and so forth. But I really believe that our participation in the outdoor actually creates more connection to each other as humans. And I think that we're seeing and experiencing that. I think that that's a very general broad case study, but I would tell you that I saw numerous times with the people that I worked with as a recreation therapist, them experiencing a couple of specific outcomes, and that's partly because we were focused on some specific outcomes and some of those outcomes were really helping them build what could easily be talked or mentioned as kind of self-confidence. But really the focus is what we call self-efficacy, building their self-efficacy, which is ultimately their belief in their ability to accomplish something or a specific task. (21:48): And we would do specific outings that challenged these kids coping mechanisms and what they believed they could or couldn't do and help them have a successful experience. And by having that successful experience, it showed them that they could overcome these specific challenges and by helping them recognize that they had what it took to overcome these challenges, we could help them take that belief in their ability to overcome that challenge and apply it to the challenges that they were working with. For example, in their therapy, their therapy with a therapist, that they were working through these other challenges, whatever they might be, and helping them direct that belief in their ability to overcome a challenge and relate it to the more personal intimate challenges that they were experiencing, whether it was in a family situation, whether it was about themselves and that we saw happen time and time again and it was like it's a beautiful, beautiful, cool process.", "token_estimate": 855, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "18:18", "end_timestamp": "18:53", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "2bedcfad-6be5-40d0-a57e-e40e54871f7c", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:08): Is that something you feel like a lot of people recognize? I mean, I'm sure we've all experienced it, right? You go out, you've never run a 5K before you go out and run the 5K and then maybe you're like, oh, I'm going to do a 10 K. Or maybe you then look at a situation at home and you're like, oh, this doesn't seem like that big of a challenge. We can work this out, we can tackle it. Do you think a lot of people, I'm sure we have all experienced that, but do you think a lot of people have been able to name that and help understand what that process is, what self-efficacy is?\n\nEli Kerr (23:42): So I don't think that majority of people, even maybe there's probably a very small amount of people that would actually label it and call it self-efficacy and that are familiar with that term. I think it's become more familiar or more popular, but I don't know about the general populace really understanding that, oh, I'm having a self-efficacy experience. Oh, my self-efficacy has increased. And really knowing and understand that it's actually a psychological theory where there's a substantial amount of research that shows that you can have and go through these experiences and that your self-efficacy in a specific area can be increased through a couple of different ways, and then that can be generalized to other areas of our life. I think some people can make that generalization on their own, but I also think that we as humans help each other sometimes make those generalizations and specifically therapists who focus on this theory really help their clients make that generalization into other areas of their life. But I don't know that we cognitively or deliberately do that. I think that there's some that it kind naturally happens, but it definitely helps to have somebody help you point it out and recognize it.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:28): Yeah. What does that do for you or an individual once you can identify that?", "token_estimate": 488, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "23:08", "end_timestamp": "25:28", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "bb598a97-f2d1-43a2-9107-9c2e7c18ab71", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Eli Kerr (25:33): Well, I think that there's a little bit of self actualization there where you start to learn that by building your self-efficacy in a specific area, that you gain more confidence slash really a belief in your ability to be able to be successful in other areas of your life too, which allows you to go after things to do things maybe that you normally wouldn't do to. I mean, I think a great example of this might be something is what we may think is as simple as applying to a job that you don't think that you have the ability or skillset to do, and so you just don't apply where because your self-efficacy increases, you would actually go apply to a job that maybe seems like it might be out of your realm, but you're still willing to try. So really trying things that you normally wouldn't try, if that makes sense.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:49): Yeah, it does. You said something there that was interesting when looking at self-efficacy, is there a difference between confidence and belief and what is that?\n\nEli Kerr (27:00): Yeah, so a couple of things. One is confidence is really associated with what you think the outcome could be, meaning it could be positive or negative. You can be very confident that you're going to fail at something. So self-efficacy is very associated with a positive outcome. So that's one specific difference. It is the belief that you will have a positive outcome in a situation. Self-efficacy also is typically is more specific in a specific task or area where confidence is more generalized.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:50): Gotcha. What got you interested in understanding, trying to understand how humans work, how humans process, where did that passion come from?", "token_estimate": 427, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:33", "end_timestamp": "27:50", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "b1e36fdc-e3ef-40ac-8634-cdb2a353a93f", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Eli Kerr (28:02): I don't know. It's interesting. I think back to when I first learned about this field of recreation therapy, I remember I was on a ski lift and I ran into a buddy that I played soccer with through college, and I hadn't seen him for years. And he's like, oh, yeah, I started this program. It's called recreation Therapy. And he's explaining, learning the recreation side and really focused in psychology. And ultimately the two things I put together, I was like, oh my God, you can do recreation and use the power of recreation and you get to help people. You get these two things and you get to marry them. It sounds perfect. I had no idea what kind of jobs were out there at that point. I had no idea what it paid. I didn't know really anything except that you got to do recreation and you got to help people. And it's like (29:02): Two things. I'm sold off things I love, so I pursued it and I got to learn a lot more about psychology and learn more about human growth. And I've always been interested with human growth and development. It's a topic that's so interesting to me. I mean, I guess there's this other component too that I'm from Utah, grew up in Utah, I grew up as a Mormon, no practicing or involved with their religion at all, but there is an aspect and principle with Mormonism that's very focused growth and very focused on development and becoming something greater. And I think that that's been really ingrained in me. And while now I'm at this point where I don't know that I fully understand or know what's going to happen to us in our lives and post life and all of that, but one thing that I still really believe and hold onto is the idea of developing as a human and becoming better and going through a process of learning, getting rid of old habits that don't serve us and taking on new ones that do. (30:44): I've always really loved that idea of the idea of progression. And I think that while I think that's interesting about what we do now at Gnarly Nutrition and my background, while my background really isn't like a typical background of say, a CEO or someone who runs a business, I think that that's kind of the beautiful part. And one of the differentiators for us at Gnarly is that because I have this pretty unique background that all of that stuff has come with me and it's become part of what we are about at Gnarly, and it's part of our brand, this idea of progression and helping people become better. And it's not about being the best, it's about being your best. It's about being better than you were yesterday. That's a component and a core belief of gnarly that we are trying to figure out how do we make this exude from the brand and so that people really feel it and are inspired by that. And that's a challenge for us because ultimately that's what we want people to see and feel from our brand.", "token_estimate": 716, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "28:02", "end_timestamp": "28:02", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "7fed1284-80b5-406f-ae5b-c23ee8343e15", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:11): I mean, you think about for an athlete, two things that they need to be their best nutrition, and then the ability to be in the right mindset and be mentally focused. And you guys, while you're selling a nutritional product, obviously you have that mental background, that mental understanding so that you can kind of combine both of them. I'm assuming as you work with your ambassador, as you market your products, as you're just there in the industry, you can kind of represent wellness on a holistic level rather than just on a nutritional level.", "token_estimate": 143, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "32:11", "end_timestamp": "32:11", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "9ed5d7af-fb77-4f0f-9117-4969f9576b5d", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Eli Kerr (32:49): Yeah, I mean, I think you bring up a really good point, and I think that what you've just shared is a good vision of who and what we want to be as a brand. It's tough to be that. I would say we want to take a really holistic approach to the athlete, to our customer and help them develop in all the ways and bring in multiple variables to help you develop and go do the things that you want to do. I'd say it's also really hard. It's hard with limited resources. It's hard to try to be all of those things, (33:38): But you're spot on. I mean, I think that would be the vision is to try to create that kind of brand and environment where we are supporting the whole athlete and not just from a nutrition standpoint. I would say that's one of the components that is a challenge and something that I've been trying to focus on as a leader in the organization. We're a small organization, but I try to build and want to have a culture internally that is really focused on what you just mentioned as far as developing our culture within the brand that really creates an environment where our team is encouraged to be their whole selves. (34:29): I definitely have a belief that creating a culture like that internally where people can come in and bring all of the chaoticness or the fullness of themselves as a person to work creates more creativity, more love and passion, more acceptance, and ultimately that is exuded from the brand. And when we can build an internal culture that exudes that, then the brand takes on those elements that we're talking about. And that's a challenge. It's a challenge that I say, I would say I don't know how to do, but one thing that's important is I believe it and it's causing me to try to be very deliberate about creating that. And I think that that deliberateness the intention is so important. It really is because I don't know how to do it, but by being deliberate about it and having that intention, you learn and you adapt and you figure shit out", "token_estimate": 502, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "32:49", "end_timestamp": "32:49", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "ebf77b28-ee25-40cc-9282-8b946169e41c", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:54): With that. If you could ask any expert on creating company culture, if you could ask them anything, what are some of your biggest questions right now as a leader, as you're trying to figure out anything top of mind?\n\nEli Kerr (36:11): That's an interesting question. We live in a pretty interesting time where I have a lot of empathy for our team, for people in general. We're all dealing with a lot of chaoticness a disruption. We're managing things that we never had to manage before, and it takes a toll on our personal lives. It takes a toll on our work lives. It takes, and we have and are dealing with so much even outside of work and in some ways more than we ever had. And so balancing that life and kind of the work and life balance is really difficult and it affects in some ways what we're able to accomplish in the work environment right now. And so as a leader, trying to really balance expectations and creating accountability for what we need to do at work and the urgency that we feel at work, but also being really empathetic with life and where it's at right now. So how do we really create this culture of accountability, but also be really empathetic with where people are at right now? It's a really difficult balance that I don't know how to figure out\n\nCole Heilborn (37:53): Because not something that you can just put together one day and roll out for the next eight years and have it thrive and have that type of culture exists. That's something you have to actively work on every day. I think probably depending on the season that you're in, the levels of accountability, the levels of empathy, those all fluctuate depending on what's going on, right?", "token_estimate": 426, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "35:54", "end_timestamp": "37:53", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "a5d88aef-cb2d-4663-a744-afa3727e2507", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Eli Kerr (38:21): Yeah. And right now we're obviously having some experiences that affect all of us fast forward years from now where we've figured this thing out and got it under control. Every one of us have more personal experiences that are, to a degree, a pandemic in our lives, something that affects us so much that we're having this disruption in our personal lives that everybody else isn't necessarily feeling. And for those individuals, how do we also have empathy for them and allow them to be dealing with and create space for them to deal with what's going on in their life, but still have that expectation and accountability for what their responsibilities at work. I really have a belief and have tried. I mean, that's one of the things that I love about what we do at Gnarly is we are all involved in the outdoors, and that's part of our lifestyle. And I try not to make this huge distinction between my work life and my personal life. I really like that it's really integrated and that work kind of goes with me in my life and it's part of my life. And same thing, life flows right into work. (39:57): I try to make a separation at times when it comes to tasks and emails, calls and so forth, and not necessarily let those bleed too much into my personal life and create some, what's the word? I don't want to use a border. (40:20): But yeah, I find that by integrating life with work and work with life, that I bring more of myself to work, kind of similar to what we were talking about a couple minutes ago where it's like I bring my whole self to work and because I bring my whole self to work, you get my emotions, but you get more of my mind and more of my creativity, and I think that that's a beautiful process and ultimately it comes out in the brand and how we communicate as a brand and what we do as a brand.", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "38:21", "end_timestamp": "38:21", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "2318b999-4e9a-4415-810f-987dae7b7435", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (41:03): Yeah, I mean, I think you're spot on there with the work that you're trying to do. For instance, with this seminar, this two month seminar that you guys are putting together right now, that's got to take so much focus, creativity, logistical handling, that you need your whole self to be present. You need the creative side. You need the logistical and left hand side of the brain to make it real simple. You need all of those elements to be front and center for not only you, but for the team just to pull off an event like this, for instance, and then extrapolate that to the direction of the company and the goals that you're setting. Again, you're not just trying to help the athlete be as fast as they can. You're trying to help them be the best as a whole as they can. In order to do that, you guys have to be as whole as you can. So yeah, I guess I don't have a question there other than just those are my thoughts.\n\nEli Kerr (42:01): Yep. Yeah, I think you're spot on. Spot on.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:06): So to bring it back to self-efficacy, are these things that when you look at these challenges, are they things they believe you can do? Or I guess how do you feel towards 'em?", "token_estimate": 302, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "41:03", "end_timestamp": "42:06", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "be36e4a3-545f-4990-8a7f-60b56ab73c07", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Eli Kerr (42:20): So yeah, I mean, I go through moments of doubt, but I also find actually sometimes my moments of doubt. That's oftentimes I deal with and work through my moments of doubt while I'm out, for example, running on trails, that's another I guess example or case study where it's like I go and find a lot of my thinking time and processing time both personally and work out in the mountains, and I will do these long runs that can be just emotional tidal waves, whether it's dealing and working through personal issues and relationship issues or work issues and my relationships at work and or problems that we're trying to solve at work. And those are moments where I'm dealing and battling with doubt. And it's amazing how oftentimes I can go and participate in a physical activity in the mountains and come back with a renewed self-efficacy, a belief and abilities. We can do this shit, we can do it, and let's rally the team and let's make it happen and work. We will be successful, work with the belief that we will be successful. And we do. We put all the effort in, and I would say that the outcome isn't always like what we would hope or as successful as we hope, but we've proven time and time again that we can come out on top, that we can be successful, and every time that you are successful, that increases your belief that you can overcome the next challenge.", "token_estimate": 347, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "42:20", "end_timestamp": "42:20", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "c3309d72-ad20-44e0-b014-26d2cc143317", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (44:43): I know we didn't get to talk a lot about marketing specifically, but I think that's okay because of the nature of the work that we do. Everyone is so creative, everyone is so forward thinking or so oftentimes introspective and we're capable of feeling and thinking these different thoughts and feelings. And so I appreciate you sharing just some of the feelings that you guys are working through and connecting that to the goals that you guys are working on. And I think kind of communicating that it's okay that maybe you don't have all the right answers to anyone. It's okay that you don't have the right answers. It's okay that you don't totally understand everything, but that's part of it, I suppose. And you just, again, one step, you accomplish one thing that builds a little bit more belief in yourself, and then you can just kind of keep climbing up whatever mountain you're climbing up.", "token_estimate": 230, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "44:43", "end_timestamp": "44:43", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "efa96e0b-a9fa-487a-b42f-12cd7a50ccb0", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Eli Kerr (45:36): Yeah, I mean, to bring this more full circle and talk a little bit about marketing, talked about some kind of very theoretical stuff and our internal culture and my beliefs as a leader and as a person and how it's impacted gnarly. But ultimately what's interesting about all of that from the marketing sense is it does influence and impact our marketing. So traditionally the fitness industry or the sports nutrition companies really focus on marketing products like ours, for example, on the aesthetics of the body and really kind of focus marketing them is almost like a magic pill. Take this (46:25): And you'll look like this. And they use sex appeal to drive a purchase of a product. We market very, very, very differently with gnarly, like I mentioned in the beginning, we are focused more on the science, on the education of what products like this do for a person, and we're more focused on teaching the athlete how to fish rather than just give them a fish. We don't believe in a magic pill. You only get progression through work, and our products don't do the work for you, but they will support the work. And so putting products like ours in the correct context is what we ultimately do for our marketing. So we're focused on the education, we're focused bringing out the science or the research around how products like ours actually help, and we want to give that information to the consumer and empower them and ultimately allow them to choose what products they need to accomplish their goals, and in giving them that empowerment, hope that they look and see us or recognize us as a brand that they can trust, that they know creates a quality product when they are ready to turn to use a product like ours. (47:57): So rather than just selling more and more and more, we ultimately market to consumers to help them be successful. We're interested in their success. Obviously we have to and need to sell product because it's what keeps us alive as a company. So we have to make those cells, we need to make those cells, and as we continue to make those cells, it generates the revenue that allows us to continue to educate, to continue to support athletes and build or communicate ways to be successful as an athlete. And that process some people really don't love or just see us as a brand or see brands as machines that are just selling stuff and there's some truth to that, but also give us a little bit more benefit of the doubt that we're selling stuff so that we can do more cool shit so that we can support people more also so that we can create a livelihood for us and our families so that we can continue to produce phenomenal products and phenomenal education, and that's what we do. And so I would say the culture and the things that we've talked about so far in our conversation really has changed the way that we market, (49:37): And it's because ultimately what are our intentions and our intentions, I really believe are in the right place. We're trying to do the right thing and that you see that in our marketing.", "token_estimate": 766, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "45:36", "end_timestamp": "45:36", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "8c2dfffa-fc66-46ab-9ca5-44366a51f51c", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (49:58): Beautiful. You summed that up wonderfully better than I could have ever had. So one of my last questions, we're running out of time. You look towards, let's say another eight years into the future. What are you excited for? What do you hope for the future of Gnarly and what are you excited for and hope for just in terms of your progression as a leader in the things that you want to learn and work through?\n\nEli Kerr (50:25): Yeah, we really want to continue making a huge influence on the outdoor industry. We really want to continue to build education that's focused on the outdoor athlete, so meaning make nutrition a larger part and a greater emphasis of on performance, how important it is on performance. And we want to continue to create a line of products that are really high quality, natural and delicious, that support the athlete in all their endeavors. So we're excited. We've got some really rad products that are coming out that continue to support the athlete from a nutrition standpoint, but we're also, we're going to be moving into other categories around nutrition, and one of the things I'm really, really excited about in the next six months is being able to provide a more fuel-based product for our outdoor athletes. And this will be a big game changer for us, and this is in line with our desire to ultimately support the athlete and providing a greater range of products. (51:46): So I mentioned that we've traditionally been kind of focused on training products, and we want to continue to build out that range of products so that we can support the athlete in whatever they're doing from a nutrition standpoint, and that's the direction that we will continue to head in the next eight years and ultimately become a greater household name for the mountain community. I think that we've done a great job with how small we've been and carving out this niche and helping to build a greater desire for outdoor athletes to be conscious of what they're putting in their bodies and how it affects their performance. And we will going to continue to do that and hopefully make a greater and greater influence on the outdoor community and ultimately be, I mean, I want us to be the sports nutrition company that outdoor athletes look to help fuel their adventures, help them be successful in whatever it is that they're wanting to accomplish, whether it's racing to individual goals, athletic goals, and that's fun. It's super fun and super rewarding.", "token_estimate": 624, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr"], "start_timestamp": "49:58", "end_timestamp": "50:25", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "db25dd30-333a-463b-9fa3-8a41d34da898", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (53:16): And for yourself?\n\nEli Kerr (53:19): For myself, I mean, I mentioned that one of the beauties of what I've gotten to do with Gnarly is that my lifestyle and work, my life and work are pretty integrated, and I love that. So I mean, haven't talked about the fact I have four kids and my four kids for good or for bad are growing up in the outdoors doing a lot of really, really cool and impactful things, and the fact that we get to go experience that stuff together is it's one of my greatest joys,\n\nSpeaker 3 (54:07): And\n\nEli Kerr (54:07): My work allows me to continue to do that, and I want to do that until I get old. I want to create those experiences with the people that I love, and I'm so grateful that I have a job, a work opportunity that allows and facilitates that. I think to the opportunity that I have to work with professional athletes and get to know them as humans and be influenced by them is also, it's amazing. It's really profound. Ultimately, one of the things that I love is, and what I really want to experience is to have gnarly grow so that I get to continue to experiment and build a culture the way that I've envisioned. Building a culture that I hope impacts me positively impacts our team members, and that's one of the really fun and exciting things for me personally as Gnarly Grows, is that our team, our internal team gets to grow and to watch people be successful and take on challenges and succeed in those challenges and the way that it influences their personal life, that shit is some of the most rewarding work that I will ever get to do. And so finding ways to better be able to do that as a leader, it is a huge focus for me.\n\nCole Heilborn (55:47): Yeah. Well, Eli, thank you. If folks want to follow along with you or gnarly, can they go to see what you guys are up to?", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eli Kerr", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "53:16", "end_timestamp": "55:47", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "aefd2a10-38eb-45b4-977d-4b34b3ae01f6", "episode_id": "2836f061-224e-4da9-8c7c-a90fc68b32d6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4850", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Eli Kerr (55:56): Yeah, so we of course do the Instagram thing. Our handle is gnarly nutrition. I'd encourage you guys to also go to go gnarly.com and you can sign up for our newsletter. The content that we push out through there is phenomenal. You get to know our athletes, what they're doing. We give you great nutrition information. But we also, like I said, like we've been talking about, we try to take a really holistic view on life in general, and I think that we are creating some phenomenal content around a lot more sophisticated aspects than just nutrition. Me personally, I'm on the Instagram too. It's a love hate relationship. I love being involved in some ways and other ways. I'm just like, I'm out.\n\nCole Heilborn (56:50): Yep, you're not the only one. Alright, well, Eli, thank you again for spending the last hour chatting. I hope you have a good rest of your day and I'm excited to see what the future holds for gnarly and for you and your own journey of self-discovery and learning. Have a good day. Take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 310, "speakers": ["Eli Kerr", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "55:56", "end_timestamp": "56:50", "podcast_index": 45, "episode_name": "Ep 45: Eli Kerr | The Whole Self | Gnarly Nutrition‬", "slug": "eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/eli-kerr-the-whole-self-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Eli Kerr", "guest_title": "CEO of Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the whole", "podcast_length": "44:67"}, {"id": "b7dd9426-506c-4bca-9d28-deaa1d11d506", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Christian Lepley (00:01): Doing some kind of ad hoc research, and I noticed that people always talked about half marathons is it's only a half marathon. No, no, it's not only, it's a half marathon. That is impressive, man. If you go around, you'll see that people do that, and I had noticed that. And so when we started, like let's just take only out of our language, everything is its own thing and it's important and a process. It's been a fun journey for us as well to really open up our eyes to all of these new opportunities and all these great people out there there.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:29): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Christian Lepley, the Vice President of marketing at Goo Energy Labs. Christian, how are you doing?\n\nChristian Lepley (01:12): Good. Great to be with you. Having a great day here in San Francisco. It's sunny and bright, so looking forward to the conversation.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:18): Likewise, I hope you guys have been having a good year so far, it sounds like. Well, the rest of the world, things have been up in the air a little bit, but it sounds like you guys got some fun stuff going on. So I'm excited to dive a little deeper into what this last year has held and what next year is looking like.\n\nChristian Lepley (01:36): Sounds good.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:36): I'm sure folks are super familiar, but let's just cover our bases. Tell us a little bit about Goo Energy, who the company is, maybe a little bit of background on its history and how it was started.", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01", "end_timestamp": "01:36", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "1193249b-5d41-496b-b45b-b5f846122a28", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Christian Lepley (01:46): Sure, yeah. Goo Energy Labs. Yeah. It started back in 1993 in Berkeley, California. Basically orbited around this idea where it was just a father trying to help a daughter run ultra marathons and not have problems with her nutrition. So being a good dad, he was into what his daughter was up to, and also being a scientist. He got back to his kitchen at home and started concocting these little experiments in nutrition for his daughter to achieve a hundred mile races in great time and with great nutrition and great performances. And so what ended up happening is he played around with different formulas. He ended up with this kind of gel-like formula that he shared with his daughter, and she shared it with her friends. And months and years later, a category was invented and a brand was born. So this happened back in 1993. Yeah, it was basically all around this idea that it was just a dad helping a daughter do something that she loved, and he didn't have the intention of creating category or a company. It was just, that's just what happened. As she started to share it around, it became a thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:49): And is that the first kind of goo energy substance that was on the market then? Was that the beginning of that category?\n\nChristian Lepley (02:57): Yeah, that was the beginning was a goo like substance that also obviously inspired the name goo. It just became goo because that's what it was, gooey, (03:04): And so they just kind of stuck with it and played around with how to phrase it and spell it, and just ended up with GU, meaning exactly what it was. And then from there, over the years, expand into other form factors, chew drinks, recovery and capsules and a bunch of other things. But essentially it was all around the gel and also the packaging of that gel both very important, what's inside the packet, and also how the packet operates and works was also very important, and it was a huge innovation as well because Goo and Dr. Bill Vaughn are the ones who invented the packet design and the functionality of the packaging as well as the ingredients on the inside.", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:46", "end_timestamp": "02:57", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "000c8585-d1b3-4339-b6a8-d8adfbc1e276", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:41): Okay, right. You got to have the easy terror access to the goo whenever you're doing whatever activity you're doing, and if you've got to fumble with it, like you do a prescription bottle, you're not actually going to be able to get the nutrients you need.\n\nChristian Lepley (03:55): Yeah, exactly. I mean, the packaging and what's inside the packaging is a piece of gear. It's how we see it. So it has to operate efficiently, it has to have durability, it has to be waterproof, it has to travel well, and all these things just like a piece of gear, a jacket or a pair of skis, it has to have the same sort of, has to be used as a tool, just like any other piece of gear in the outdoors. So that's how it was looked upon. So a lot of thought and innovation went into the packaging as well as what's on the inside of the packaging. And so we try to do that with all the new form factors that we have developed over the years. It's all about the ingredients, but also about how you use those ingredients and the portability of the packaging and the convenience of the packaging and all those things. Because just like a Gore-Tex jacket, it can save your life out there if it's operating properly. So it's a piece of gear in our minds.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:44): Sure. So as the VP of marketing, I guess, how long have you been with the company and what does your role entail?\n\nChristian Lepley (04:51): Yeah, it's coming on four years in April. Next year it'll be four years at Goo. And as the head of marketing, I basically have two pillars to my job. The first one is I have the opportunity to manage a crazy band of super innovative and talented marketing people where we cover off social media, web content, public relations, packaging design, event activations, anything that has anything to do with communicating with our community internally and externally. My team does, and I have the privilege of managing them. So that's the one pillar and the second pillar as being on the executive team, I also help drive corporate strategy for the future of GOO and all our sub-brands. So I have those two basic, basic pillars, manage marketing, and then be on the leadership team.", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "03:41", "end_timestamp": "04:51", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "cad937a9-e74e-49d2-a27c-b7c88b9edf1a", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:34): Okay. Very cool. And prior to goo, you were working where?\n\nChristian Lepley (05:40): Yeah. Prior to go, I as a consultant, I worked for ad agencies and directly for brands as a brand consultant, strategy consultant. Did that for about a decade, just bouncing around, working with brands like the North Face and Levi's and a few other fashion brands, and also in the healthcare industry. I did some things for them, just kind on ad hoc basis, kept myself busy, just bouncing around from place to place. But before that, I was full-time staffer, ad ad agencies, car accounts, fashion accounts, all kinds of things, and then just branched out on my own so I could climb and travel and run and do all my things while also working. So that worked for about a decade, and then when this opportunity to go came up, I just could not pass up the chance to spend all my time with my people doing one thing, focusing on one brand and doing what I love. So it was a great opportunity. It's been great every single day for four years now.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:28): Yeah. I'm curious, slightly off topic, but you mentioned that you decided to do the consulting, jump out on your own so that you could pursue those activities you loved. I feel like a lot of people do that and then they get sucked into doing all the work and not actually going out and doing the climbing, where they're running or whatever it is they're into. Were you able to manage that relationship well for yourself?", "token_estimate": 374, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "05:34", "end_timestamp": "06:28", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "63e249d6-b1d2-477f-931b-9ea3bcf47cfb", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Christian Lepley (06:48): Yeah. One of the great features and benefits of working at Goo is it has an incredible work-life balance. This comes down from the top, our owner, Brian Vaughn, he the CEO who runs the company, he believes in a work-life balance. He does it himself, and he allows all of us, all of the employees to do the same. So I didn't slow down a bit. I became more structured for sure, because I couldn't take phone calls from a beach in Croatia on the way to a climbing spot or something like that, and I couldn't have meetings up on Mount Tam on a Wednesday afternoon. But because of the balance, I'm able to kind of do all those things just in a different time slot. So actually I think I was kind of ready to have a little more structure in my life, so I appreciated the fact that I was able to do both, but just a little bit more on a schedule. And so it's fine. Most of our meetings, half the crew is in a bike kit or running shorts or something, so everybody's kind of on their way in or on their way out (07:43): As we're doing our work. So for me, it wasn't much of a change. I just had to keep track of my time a little bit differently than I had in the past.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:49): Sure. Sure. I feel like it's a good time for us to connect and chat on the podcast today, because it's currently beginning of December. This episode's going to come out later in 2021, but I've been doing a few of these podcast interviews. It seems like we've been doing a lot the last couple weeks, and it's been fun to connect and chat with brands as they're wrapping up this year. So I'm excited to chat today and learn a little bit more about what you guys have gone through in the last year, the opportunities that have come out of it, maybe some of the initiatives or things that you're looking for for 2021. Can you just kind of paint us a picture of this year and what next year is forecasting to look like?", "token_estimate": 481, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:48", "end_timestamp": "07:49", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "2a2b266c-85cc-43a1-8d99-8f9937b9382a", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Christian Lepley (08:29): Sure. Yeah. I mean, like everybody on the planet, the most unique year we probably all of us have ever experienced in Q1 of 2020. Things were sailing along great. We were having a great beginning of the year, had no idea what was about to happen, but we were feeling super optimistic. We had a bunch of new products in the pipeline. We had our marketing programs all set ready to roll, and everybody was super excited, super optimistic, but then all of a sudden things just changed. We're out of the office, we're working from home, everything's shut down. All of our channels for selling. Our products basically shut down. The events went away, our athletes got confused, our community was confused, what do we do now? And so we had to really make some very quick decisions about what to do about our operation and our company. (09:11): We immediately put everybody remote, made some adjustments, reduced our spending, because immediately our sales were impacted in many of our channels. And so we just spent the first few months just kind of refocusing and re-engineering how we're going to operate, knowing that we wanted to keep going and keep supporting our communities, but it took us maybe 30, 30 to 60 days to kind of figure that, figure all that out, thanks to technology. It was super simple and a lot of us travel a lot, so we were kind of used to doing things on the road anyhow. So for the marketing and sales teams and a few other teams, it wasn't a big change as it was for maybe somebody in r and d or in accounting or other really important departments. They just weren't used to being at home all the time. So it was a different experience for all the different team members at goo. (09:54): But for us in sales and marketing, it was pretty easy, but it was also kind of shocking that all of a sudden everything had changed. So we made that pivot and that was great. And then we started to see the smoke started to settle a little bit in terms of, okay, what are we looking at on the horizon? And so at that moment, we had to figure out how do we support our community? And our community is not only, it's people that work at Goo, it's our retailers, it's the race directors that we're partners with, and it's of course our athletes and our consumers. How do we work with them to help them? We understood that maybe sports nutrition isn't the number one thing on their minds right now, especially if their training for races has stopped. Retailers were shut down or only delivering shoes out on the curb, all these kinds of things. (10:38): So we spent a lot of time focusing on the community because really GOO was born basically to support a community. It wasn't, like I mentioned before, it wasn't intended to be a company. It was intended to support people. And so everything we do is for the community. We orbit it around our communities. So we really pointed our lens on, okay, what can we do to help all the various communities that we're involved in, and especially race directors, where their races suddenly went away, and then they tried to flip to virtual races. And so one of the initial instincts was maybe, well, races are over, races are canceled. No, they're not canceled. They're just virtual. So it doesn't have to be a live event. You don't have to be together to have it be a live event. So we had to shift our focus and our thinking about how we get products to people, how we sample products, how much sports nutrition do you need If you're just doing a virtual on your own own timeframe, maybe not as much, but still, it'd be better if you're out there with some nutrition that's going to keep you rolling along in a good way. (11:32): So we just shifted everything as how we saw the world. We just said the communities are the same, the world is different. So that was actually a relatively easy shift for us, but we had to kind of go down the line for every discipline and make sure our communities are the same, but the world is different. So how do we support those communities in this changed world and know that it might be permanent, it might be semi-permanent, or it might go away in three weeks. We didn't really know. So we've just been rolling with it ever since. So for us, it was a great challenge, an interesting challenge, a little nerve wracking in the beginning for many folks. They were like, wow. People had certain disciplines that seemed to just go away, like event activators. Suddenly there's no events. What do you do? It's not what you do, it's how you see what you do. (12:15): It's like how do you view it? And so we just flipped the script and everybody's happy, and we kept things going. And now we've learned a lot about virtual events. We've learned different ways of sampling products. We've learned how to support people when we know they don't need as much nutrition as they may have needed before when they were in a serious training block. So we learned how to be more empathetic. We learned how to increase the size of our tent and bring in new athletes and more athletes. So that was an opportunity that I can get to in a bit is like we learned a lot and we've also see opportunity, even though business has changed and the size of the category has changed and things are uncertain for next year, but we see a lot of opportunity in the future, especially in 21, where things are going to be a mixed bag, but we know what it is this time where it's like in 20, we didn't know it was even coming. (13:05): It just happened. And so now we know what it is and like, okay, we can do this. And so our team is very unique in a sense. The optimism and the marketing team is incredible, and they always look for opportunities. They disregard the problems and just look for the opportunities, which I think is a unique character trait. I think it's nurtured at goo as a culture. I'm sure they come to it with that ethos born into them, but it's nurtured at goo, and I try to keep that going, that things are not a problem. They're opportunities, and if there is a real, real problem, we deal with it and then we move on. So it's been a great opportunity for a lot of folks. It's been hard. We've lost some teammates. We've had to shrink the size of the company and do things like that, but also it's been an amazing growth opportunity for those of us that are at goo.", "token_estimate": 1591, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "08:29", "end_timestamp": "08:29", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "86dca1ff-21d0-4c43-a37b-76f43ef2639c", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:51): Yeah, I mean, that's a super healthy way to look at it, and I feel like that's the only way you can look at it if you really want to get through it in a good way and come out ahead or just even, because I feel like that's all we're really trying to do is just maintain where we were. Talk about some of those opportunities, what came out of it, what were some of the surprises that has come out of the last nine months for you, and now you're looking at and saying, oh, shoot, we can do this for 2021?", "token_estimate": 131, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:51", "end_timestamp": "13:51", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "dce3c7aa-a398-45fd-aa72-3fe6c9061a2d", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Christian Lepley (14:20): Yeah, yeah. I mean, one of the big revelations was for us, I don't know, for folks who know goo pretty well, they know that we have a high standard of performance. We like to be on podiums and we like to finish strong, and we like to win races, but we also mostly like to have fun doing it, but there's no joke. We might be smiling, but we do want to win. And so with that comes a certain, I don't know, a high standard elitism or something where like, oh, it is only for the super committed athlete and those kinds of products. They're not for me and maybe don't really need, if I'm running a 10 K or my first half marathon or I'm riding my bike for the first time over 20 miles or swimming across a channel or something, you don't really need that. (15:00): It's overthinking it. I'm sure there was. I know that a certain segment of the athletic population didn't really connect with the brand because that's what they thought of the brand of like, well, it seems cool, but I don't really need it. And we didn't necessarily not think that either because we were like, well, really, we focus on run bike and try and we try to do the best we can and enhance performances, great training, great performances, have a good time, achieve personal best, f kts, whatever it is. But it was always about have a good time, but do your best. It wasn't as casual, not as casual as it maybe it could have or should have been, but the pandemic slowed everything down. It slowed down our elites, it slowed down our committed athletes, our big broad consumer base, but then it also introduced us to a whole segment of the population that was either getting back into athleticism or exercise food for the first time or for the first time in a long time. (15:53): And so I had the opportunity to sit down really quietly with our team to say, and our owners, how do we talk to these folks? They are now part of our community. I would go out my door, my house where I live in San Francisco, and I saw more people out on the roads than I've ever seen in the years I've lived here. And I thought, wow, this is interesting. I'm sure a certain percent of them, they will stop doing it once they go back to work and go back to the office, but a lot of 'em are not. They're creating new habits here. So how do we support that? Not in a way that we want to force products on them that they don't necessarily need, but how do we re-engineer our messaging and our communications around, Hey, if you're running a 5K in the morning or an actual 5K virtual race or a race, no, you don't need to carry a bunch of nutrition products with you, but you probably want to gel on your way out the door. (16:42): It's going to be better for you than a banana and a glass of water that you might have eaten before. And that certainly is fine, a banana and a glass of water, but actually an energy gel will do a lot better for you. You'll process it better. You'll have a better performance, you'll feel good, you'll recover better. All these things, you actually could benefit from that, but it's really only one gel. You don't need to buy all of the products and the whole lineup. It's just really about that. So we had to learn how to gently bring people into this notion that nutrition is important for everyday movement because ultimately goo as a company, we orbit around our community, but what we want our community to do is move. So we're all about supporting and feeding people's need to move every day. So if that is our brand mission, then of course we care about athletes of all levels and of all experiences in any activity, whether it's soccer on weekends or if it's training for an ultra marathon or for your first century or for swimming to Alcatraz or whatever it is, we care about all those people. (17:48): So it really opened up our eyes and opened up our opportunity as a brand to communicate to anybody who wants to move. That I think is an opportunity that in 21, we're really going to lean into that. We're going to obviously stay committed to our core and a lot of brands, as you've, I'm sure heard in all the different conversations you've had with the core and more challenge, certainly we have it as well, and I don't think that we necessarily knew that we had it before the pandemic, but now that we've had a chance to really communicate and bond with more athletes, we realize, yeah, we definitely support our elite athletes and are really committed athletes that want to want to achieve personal best every time they go out. But we also really care deeply about folks who just want to move through their neighborhood, through their local park along the beach when they're on vacation, they want to run and go from here to there or ride from here to there. We care about those folks. So again, I think the opportunities that opened our eyes to that, and then also we had to learn how to communicate with them in a way that was not intimidating, but was empathetic to where they're at and what their goals are. Because we don't want to force them to achieve personal best every time they go out if that's not what they want, but we want them to be healthy and happy and feel good when they're done.", "token_estimate": 1304, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "14:20", "end_timestamp": "14:20", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "b40f34f0-6565-4fd5-bdc6-e9ec09a05dd2", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:58): How do you communicate with them? If you've kind of built the brand and the company has been built on this, as you said, kind of this elite athlete mentality, I got to imagine for a consumer who's just getting out for an evening jog, it could be pretty intimidating to have a brand that they're not really familiar with, or a brand that is promoting Olympians and triathletes to then show up on their doorstep and be like, Hey, have you ever considered having a goo? And they're like, what on earth is a goo? How have you talked to them and you, I guess, what have you learned and have had to change in how you communicate?", "token_estimate": 162, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:58", "end_timestamp": "18:58", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "bb55420d-0748-49fb-beb9-8567875d6455", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Christian Lepley (19:39): Yeah, that's a good question. I think because part of our communication style, when we talk to committed elite athletes, it's a science-based science-backed product and also philosophy. (19:50): So there's a lot of science and a lot of technology that goes into how we formulate our products and including the packaging. So that stuff is important and that's why it works and that's why it's popping. That's why it's been around for 27 years. So that's important. However, I don't think it resonates with these other athletes. It's not going to be as important to somebody who's just really wanting to do really well running three or four miles in the mornings or actually running a 5K that's been organized. So what I have worked with the team on is just okay know that we have that science DNA, but that's not how we can speak to these folks. We have to have empathy. So for example, an energy gel, rather than giving somebody a nutrition plan for an entire four hours out on course or in the water or wherever it is, it can be as simply as we make 31 flavors of these energy gels. (20:45): There's got to be one in there that you love, from birthday to salted watermelon to whatever it may be, tastefully nude, which has basically no flavor. All of these, there's 31 of them, and they're made for fun and they're made. So you mix up your flavor profile as you're out every day or for long days. It gives you variety. Well pick a great flavor that if you like chocolate cake, then put a chocolate energy gel on your shoes in the morning, just one gel on your shoes. So when you go out the door, eat the gel, go out and have a great time. You don't have to do anything else, gel glass of water, and then you're done. So it's just that simple. Instead of having a bagel or a banana or a bowl of cereal, that is fine. It's calories, it's great, except it's not the right kind of calories in the right formulation. So the gels are crafted for that. And so if you eat one of these, it tastes great and then you're done. It's the simplicity of it, (21:38): It's the ease of it, and it's the taste of it. So I might want to, as a marketing person, lean more into the convenience and taste of it rather than the science of it, because our elite athletes care about the science and the taste. They love to eat well, but really it's about that science first. It's not science first. With these other athletes, it's really convenience and that it works and then it tastes good. So it's the tone by which you speak to them. So that's what we had to kind of teach ourselves that. It's not always about the nerdy science. It's really about does this stuff just work? Because I think people's eyes will gloss over if you start to get too involved in the science and the technology that's gone into it, especially when you're talking about amino acids and things like that. I just know that they're good for you and they'll help you recover, and you'll have more fun out", "token_estimate": 746, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "19:39", "end_timestamp": "19:39", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "89591916-e529-4105-8ac8-6f55f2f1e7fc", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Speaker 3 (22:23): There.\n\nChristian Lepley (22:24): Your playlist will sound better, your feet will feel great, your legs will recover faster. All those things are great, but you get out of eating a tasty chocolate energy gel. So it's that kind of conversation that we have. It's more of a conversation than a lecture. It's really about, hey, just like a friend would say, have you tried this? So my visual is just ask them to put an energy gel on their shoes in the morning. If they run in the mornings, they're in the dark, kind of get ready, put it on their shoes, or put it on the bike seat, grab it, eat it, drink a glass of water out the door, and you're good for your hour.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:53): So\n\nChristian Lepley (22:53): It's that kind of conversation that we have with them.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:55): How have you, because assuming, maybe I'm assuming this too simplistically, but last year your audience was the mostly elite, and now you've expanded your audience to everyone it sounds like, who's just interested in being active. Those weren't people though that you had previously identified or even really knew who they were, what they were doing, where they were living. How have you found these people now that you're working with and including in your community? Does that make sense?\n\nChristian Lepley (23:29): Yeah. One way is that we have gotten involved in sponsoring 5K races, virtual races, which we wouldn't necessarily do unless it was part of a bigger package. If there was a marathon, half marathon and the 5K was a part of it, yeah, it was great and we would support that. But we have actually sponsored specifically 5K. Only\n\nCole Heilborn (23:48): People", "token_estimate": 422, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Christian Lepley", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:23", "end_timestamp": "23:48", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "1e5402de-9603-4f50-8d6f-ec9a96c115ee", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Christian Lepley (23:48): Are out doing their things or get involved in Turkey trots for fun, and then create marketing communications and social media engagements that involve invite your friends and family into what you're doing. If you're our follower and you're a more committed athlete, and so you know what it is and you're a part of it, but you have friends who have been kind of flirting with the idea, don't really understand why you ride your bike every day, but are interested. We've invited them to invite them in. So we have found these channels by which we can communicate with those folks through things that already exist. So we didn't invent a channel. We just went to think places where what was already happening. We just weren't leaning into it as heavily as we could have in the past. We didn't really need to or thought there was a reason to, but once we got a quick look at them, it was like, well, there's a need out there and we can really support them and their goals. So that's, and it'll just roll from there. We do it organically. We do a grassroots because everything we pretty much do is grassroots start out in the field and then build up from there. We don't want to come in from the top down and it won't seem genuine to us. We're not going to sponsor a soccer team first and then get into, no, it's like the start with where people are just playing soccer and having a good time, and then we'll build up from there. So\n\nSpeaker 3 (25:00): It's\n\nChristian Lepley (25:01): Those kinds of things that we just kind of hunted down and went after, and we'll continue to do that for years to come. I'm sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:07): Is there any concern that with this new angle or with this new approach or this wider perspective, I guess, that you lose or alienate some of your committed core athletes?", "token_estimate": 458, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:48", "end_timestamp": "25:07", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "a39d0128-7ce5-4247-bd89-30b30b7f1328", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Christian Lepley (25:23): No, I don't believe so because our core athletes, they're very much what we have noticed about whether it's run by try or outdoor, all of those categories, they're very social, very friends and family focused and care about communities and care about the environment and care about all of these things that really involve a wider community. So it feels like it's a natural growth of the brand, (25:51): And we would never recommend using goo products in a way that isn't involve movement. So even though it tastes really good, don't eat a gel or a chew at your desk. That's not what they're for. There's ingredients in there that are not a good diet if you're not moving. So we feel that just by that nature, it won't alienate the core because we're not going to do anything that would make the product seem like it isn't for what the committed athlete's doing as well, if that makes sense. Sure. You could make a jacket if you're an outdoor brand and you make a jacket that was for climbing, say, K two, and it was inspired by a K two climb, and it has all of this technology about that kind of extreme atmosphere. And then it is for sale and marketed at Nordstrom as a jacket. That's great for walking down the street to get a latte on a cold day, even though many people buy them for that. If it was marketed way that would hurt the core. We are not taking that approach. It's all about very natural, just bringing in more athletes into the tent, and I've never run across any goo athlete or goof fan that was not for bringing more people into the movement tent, so to speak. (26:59): Sure. So yeah, it's a careful balance, but I think it's a genuine, authentic balance. It's not like we're, again, we're not changing formulations, we're not doing anything differently. We're just bringing more people into the party. And so I think that, yeah, trail runners especially are just such a friendly group of people that they want more people at the party, and so that's just all we're doing is helping more people get to the party.", "token_estimate": 514, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "25:23", "end_timestamp": "25:23", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "4c74b3f9-5c0a-4b8e-a78a-529598cb7dee", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (27:20): Yeah. Awesome. What about the influx of folks enjoying the outdoors in general? I mean, you mentioned people are out doing more running in the urban environment or getting out on their bikes on the greenway trails, but there's also a bunch of people who are getting into camping who are maybe getting into mountain biking, skiing, climbing, et cetera. Are you guys looking at those demographics and those targets as well and kind of approaching this the same way with them?", "token_estimate": 124, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:20", "end_timestamp": "27:20", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "a4161d6e-4cb7-4708-a4c7-f2677ec0d699", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Christian Lepley (27:52): Yeah, I like talking about this because it's really interesting. I mentioned earlier about, I was talking about goo as a piece of gear, and one of the things that we're putting a lot of time into is when it comes to folks who camp, hike, Boulder, swim or committed swimmers across channels, spelunking, all these things in the outdoors that you can think of that involve movement for a long time and energy is that we, because I have said we have primarily focused on run bike and try and a little bit of outdoor over these 27 years, but outdoors has always been a part of it because a lot of things that we do are in the outdoors. So we've always had a connection with people who are doing different things out there, but we've kind of focused on those four areas. So in the future, what we're doing is we're creating communications and partnerships with those athletes and with brands and in those channels where we're able to educate them about sports, nutrition and goose specifically. (28:52): The packet, for example, is a piece of gear because the way that it's made, it's not just what's on the inside, but it's that packaging. Because if you're a skier or a surfer, surfing for example, is wonderful. You're out in a lineup for a couple hours. One of the things that brings you back to shore is hunger and exhaustion, because if you're a committed surfer, you'd rather just live out there and just surf every single minute of your day if you could, but you can't. So the gels are waterproof. They can go inside a wetsuit and you can have yourself a gel within just a couple of seconds, and then you're back in the lineup riding down waves like you, you just arrived. So there's all kinds of things, but it, it's how the athletes and those consumers see how the products work, see it as a piece of gear. (29:38): So it's really about the education around how this stuff works and how convenient it really is, versus you can't bring a sandwich out into the water or you can't necessarily carry something like that around with you. So people have tried and it doesn't really work. So I think there's huge opportunity for the brand to speak to all of those audiences with the existing products, but just again, being empathetic to what their situation is and what their scenario is. And then I think a lot of it will have to do with that, see sports nutrition as a piece of gear, not as just a piece of food, because food is not really maybe the high priority for them. But when you think about those packets, for example, the jail packets, man, they are convenient for a lot of those things. Skiing, I mean, it's so small and tasty and boom, it's quick headed energy, you can keep going. So it applies to all of those activities. We just haven't chosen to focus on them for most of the time we've been around.", "token_estimate": 711, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "27:52", "end_timestamp": "27:52", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "08a79675-fc7c-4254-a4a2-80c88faac5fc", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (30:31): Why is it that your average outdoors person maybe doesn't, let's say they're a skier and they don't think about, why don't they think about nutrition in the way that a trail runner might when they're on the slope for nine or 10 hours? Where's that disconnect?\n\nChristian Lepley (30:47): Yeah, I think the disconnect is that sports nutrition has generally been marketed as something only for competition and only for, you have to, but you don't want to, it doesn't taste good enough. It's not real food. It's not these things. So when you're skiing, for example, especially if it's resort skiing, yeah, I mean, gosh, a bowl of chili over some fries sounds pretty good when it's a cold day. And so it's all about the food. It's about what the food is, not what it does for you. (31:12): So I think it's changing over time. And you've seen even just in the Formula one world, the auto racers where they weren't always athletes like they are today, in their early stages of auto racing, car racers were not necessarily athletes. They didn't train, they didn't work out. They just were really about driving a car really well and having the fastest engine. But nowadays, they're all elite athletes. They know the benefit of being fit and being able to have endurance. And so when it comes to a resort skier, it's kind of the same thing. It's like, well, yeah, it's great to have a cold beer and a chili dog at lunch. However, it doesn't really allow you, it doesn't give you the fuel to just crush those last few turns at the end of the day, which most people really want to do. So again, it's the communication and just reminding folks that this is what this stuff is formulated for, and it's all for good. (32:02): It's all for fun. It's all to help you achieve everything that you've ever wanted to achieve in the outdoors and with your athleticism. So I wouldn't sit here and say that it's going to replace the enjoyment of a meal you could get at a resort restaurant, but the priority can be a little bit more in balance where like, Hey, actually maybe it doesn't replace your lunch, and that is not the intention. It doesn't replace your lunch. Your lunch could be more nutritious. And we have a whole ethos about when you eat in between training racing, but while you're out on the slopes, these are great to keep you going in a way that you want to keep going. They're not going to tire you out and you're not going to bonk. So it is all about the education and about, oh, I didn't realize how handy that is. Not saying don't have a cold beer at the end of a day. It's just while you're out there doing it, this stuff works great for that and it tastes good.", "token_estimate": 671, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "30:31", "end_timestamp": "30:47", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "1d812aea-8da7-4ef9-9137-fde24b71dd4f", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:50): How do you educate? I mean, you're talking about educating someone about nutritional habits, that's not just something that you read an article about online and then completely change your lifestyle. That's a very big part of, or their day to day. How do you go about influencing, how do you go about educating in a way that, I guess, how do you structure that?\n\nChristian Lepley (33:16): Yeah. Well, we've structured it first and the ground. The foundation is our ambassador program. We have an ambassador program called Salty Squad,\n\nSpeaker 3 (33:23): And", "token_estimate": 146, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "32:50", "end_timestamp": "33:23", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "6e89bcf2-b493-4f53-8f47-47c79ee281df", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Christian Lepley (33:24): They are like all ambassador teams. They're put together, assembled each year and refined each year, and our folks, our goof people out in the field, sharing the Go love and the goo ethos and what sports nutrition can do for you in your everyday activities. So they're kind of the number one messengers, not only on the ground, but then also through their feed. So we use them as the great sort of, they democratize this conversation about nutrition when you're running around doing stuff. So they're number one. And then on our website and through our social feeds that we manage, we also spend a lot of time just talking about using this, educating folks on what sports nutrition can do for you and what its purpose in your life can be. So we use our channels, we use our ambassadors, we use our pro team as well. (34:17): They also do a great job in just speaking in regular terms to their audience. And then at events, when there's friends and family supporting other folks at events, we talk to everybody. When there was live events, we would talk to anybody who came up and What is this stuff? Because a lot of people would come and say, what is a gel? Well, we'll show you. And so we just do it one at a time. We don't spend a lot of money. A lot of brands aren't category on paid advertising. We do a fair bit, but that's not the thrust of it. It's really about one-to-one conversations with the community. Again, everything that we do kind of starts with the community, and that's where it usually ends. So it's like we use our community to spread it and we want to grow organically. So we're not in any rush. (35:01): We want to do it in the right way. We want to do things intentionally, not just because it's a revenue gain, it's really about that intentional growth. And so we do things in the right way. We do have also a responsibility that they're not snacks, they, it is sports nutrition, so we have to do it carefully, but it's just, again, grassroots conversations and the word just spreads because really you're going to take a recommendation from your friend or family about an energy jail that looks weird to you rather than from an ad, you're just not going to trust an ad. I wouldn't be like, I'm not going to do that for the first time that AD told me to. I want somebody to tell me about it. So it's perfect for just a conversation, and that's what we do is we just have conversations with people and we have patience, and we don't need to double in size in a year. We'll do it for as long as it takes. It doesn't really matter to us as long as we can have those meaningful conversations.", "token_estimate": 660, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "33:24", "end_timestamp": "33:24", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "63573ee5-99cb-403a-83c6-3e531126256c", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:51): Does it seem, I don't know, does it seem odd or not odd, that's not the right word. I feel like this year the curtains have been kind of pulled back for you guys, and going into this year, last year you were focused, you knew what you were doing. 2020 was all planned out, and then everything, it all kind of fell apart. But with that, I feel like it kind of sounds like your perspective and the curtains have been opened up and now you guys have seen this whole new piece to the world that you can now start to serve and include and build a community. Does it seem odd or does it seem, I just feel like that's a strange, out of this pandemic has come this interesting new perspective for the company that sounds like it's kind of changed everything that you guys are doing and will continue to do into the future. I dunno, just curious to hear your thoughts reflecting on that.", "token_estimate": 226, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "35:51", "end_timestamp": "35:51", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "b4191b49-9eec-4135-a77e-2fccc45d4017", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Christian Lepley (36:52): It was like an awakening that the word for many folks, it was an awakening that really helped to land and solidify our brand mission, which has always been feed the need to move. It's always been about movement. But we did look at it a bit with blinders in terms of, well, it's performance and it's long distance things and it's really committed athletes and it's really about pushing your body and pushing your performances every day all the time up until race day and through race day. And that's how we were born. And that is a lot of people that work at Guo are elite athletes. It's really intimidating on Monday morning to come into the office and hear the whispers of the podiums. Like, oh boy, wow. So there's a lot of that. So I think the pandemic allowed us to take a deep breath and have that awakening of like, wow, there's a lot of people out there that really could benefit from the knowledge that we have, the experience that we have and the products that we make in a way that's like I mentioned before, but it is empathetic to that. (37:48): They don't need everything that we make and they don't need it in the quantities that we always, but they could benefit from it and they could just have more fun out there. If that's all it is, the goal is to have more fun sports nutrition from any brand, we'll do that for you. So we advocate for eating well in between training, racing, and then while you're out there doing it using these products, because it does just allow you minimally to have more fun. And that's like they say, the best runner is the one that's having the most fun or the best cyclist, the one that's having the most fun. And we truly believe that even though, like I said before, we do want to win, but also it allowed us to really kind of gain some empathy and some perspective and look at the world in a much bigger way. (38:30): We always loved everybody, but we just didn't really think about it. It wasn't top of mind us until we were kind of forced to do it and wow, this is actually great and we love this, but in no way are we abandoning our science-based, science backed win races do well, get fts, do the crossing, do the whole thing, run to the moon, all that stuff we still believe in. But they exist together. They're not against each other. They exist together. And we truly really believe that it's just in moderation. If you're an athlete that's only, it's doing 5K, it's not only a 5K because we don't even want to use that word only anymore. We're taking that out out of the language. It's not only a 5K, it's a 5K, man. That's something. It's a half marathon. I had doing some kind of ad hoc research, and I noticed that people always talked about half marathons is it's only a half marathon. No, no, it's not only, it's a half marathon. That is impressive, man. That is a big deal, and that is really fun and really impressive. It's a half marathon, not only a half marathon. If you go around, you'll see that people do that. And I had noticed that. And so then we started, let's just take only out of our language. Everything is its own thing and it's important and impressive.", "token_estimate": 790, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "36:52", "end_timestamp": "36:52", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "2d2252eb-9ea6-4fbf-9a00-03367ff61fbc", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:47): Interesting.\n\nChristian Lepley (39:47): Yeah, it's been a fun journey for us as well to really open up our eyes to all of these new opportunities and all these great people out there that really could benefit from what we do.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:56): So as the guy who helps manage the team, obviously we hope that another pandemic doesn't hit, but obviously some good things that have come out it for you guys. How do you ensure moving forwards that yourself or the team don't miss other opportunities that are out there or other segments that are out there who you guys could serve moving forwards without another pandemic hitting and drawing the curtains back? Has that changed how you guys internally look at, I guess, the world and how you do your job?", "token_estimate": 195, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "39:47", "end_timestamp": "39:56", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "e7cd06dc-2e55-4560-9572-e766411a1fbe", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Christian Lepley (40:33): And I think this will answer the question, but I think as I've talked about an awakening, but it's also an awakening with an awareness that we only have so many resources and only so many eyeballs to do things. So we're awake, but we're also going to remain focused that we can't do everything all at once because that'll be disingenuous for one thing. It'll just be like you're just spreading things out just because it's there. No, so we're being careful about who we communicate with and who we're starting to have conversations with in terms of new athletes. So it's awake, but also aware that let's do this sustainably because again, from the top down, it's always about sustainable growth, doing things for the right reasons, smart revenue versus dumb revenue, so to speak. And so it's just being really deliberate and careful and authentic about it. (41:22): So it's kind of one at a time. So we're not going to just assume that everybody in the world that moves needs this. I may have said that, but that's not really what we mean in terms of how we're going to deploy our resources. So it's a really good question because otherwise we'll be spread too thin and then we'll lose focus, and then you become something else, and then you kind of just become flat and you kind of disappear. So we do have to remain, remember our core and our heritage and our athletes and our committed athletes and all that, but then it's like just talk to people one at a time and then know when you've had too much, and then you have to slow it down and just keep an eye on it because yeah, you'll become really scattered as a brand and then also as a team in terms of their daily jobs would be really difficult. (42:04): And sometimes it does come up and I mean, when I watch our team, our marketing team dialogue on one of our various ways of communicating these days, and I can see things kind of bouncing around ideas, and sometimes we do need to reign it in. It's like, okay, interesting, but let's reign it back in. Let's remember how, okay, good reminder, good to riff, good to have a nice little brainstorm session, but let's bring back to where we are and then we can put that on the shelf or bring that in and introduce that later. So we just keep an eye on it. We want to open it up and talk about anything anything's possible, but then bring it in and then let's stay on point because we're a famous company, but a small company and we have to keep our resources in check.", "token_estimate": 625, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "40:33", "end_timestamp": "40:33", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "2bdff0ad-dde4-4872-9414-16360d54ee2f", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (42:43): Sure. So I feel like we've talked a lot about the opportunities that have come out of this year. With those opportunities though, what are the biggest challenges? What are the biggest hurdles that you guys are trying to figure out how to overcome?\n\nChristian Lepley (42:58): As we became awake and aware? One things, one of the things I was kind of wondering about, and I sort of realized along the way, was that, boy, sports nutrition is a luxury item of all these things I've said in this conversation about how important nutrition is with your performances, because a banana and water will get used only so far. Truth is people are creating new habits when they don't have as many events to plan for or if they're just stuck at home or if they've lost their jobs. Sports nutrition isn't necessarily cheap to buy. If you're an athlete, it adds up over a training block or over a year. There's a fair bit of money that goes into just this item alone. And so it was an interesting realization that we are kind of a luxury item for many people. And then how do we remain relevant after all this is done? (43:49): People have created new habits. They may have lost their jobs or have a reduction in their jobs, or realized that, heck, I don't really need to spend money on that. I want to spend it somewhere else. So how do we, we bring back those what we thought were healthy habits, healthy for their athleticism, but also healthy for our company, of course. But we believe in what we do and what we sell. So our challenge, I think, is to break some of the habits that have been created and bring 'em back to where they were before the pandemic in terms of eating well, having the right nutrition on course, doing all the right things so you achieve what you want to achieve. So I think it's a challenge because I think admittedly I would say, what if I was paying for this stuff? Would I continue to pay for it? (44:33): I don't know if I didn't work at goo, would, I don't know. It depends. It depends on what I was exposed to during the period of the pandemic and then how I'm feeling, what my job situation was after the pandemic. So we have to be aware of that, and that is a challenge for us because the size of everything has shrunk. Will it go back? Well, we feel like it will because there's a lot of love for the brand. People are rearing up to get back to live events. We hope that they are. We feel like they would be once they come back in whatever shape form they come back in, but did they create new habits that don't involve us? It's a challenge. So we saw a huge opportunity to talk to more people, but then also the people that we have been partnering with and talking to as a consumer base, they may have moved on. We don't know. And so I think it's a challenge for us because it does open the curtain to use your words that, oh, nutrition, I didn't really need that. I didn't really, really need it. Well, it's arguable, right? It's like a Gore-Tex jacket. You don't really need Goretex for what it's made for. Nobody really ever uses Gore-Tex, the technology at its capacity. I don't think anybody ever gets to that level. If they have, they probably no longer need a jacket. (45:41): So you could put that filter onto sports nutrition as well. If you're a consumer like, well, gosh, I learned how to make these little rice cakes and I've done this and I've done that, and it's actually kind of fun. It's more interesting and more personal. I think I'm just going to stick with that. That will be unfortunate for us. But we'd like people to be conservative in their spending and be appropriate, but we also feel our formula can really help people. But yeah, habits have been formed, and so we have to gently undo some of those habits, and I think that will be a challenge. I believe it won't be easy.", "token_estimate": 962, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "42:43", "end_timestamp": "42:58", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "fd67a6e6-df27-4896-b870-e1f47bcc8b5b", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Cole Heilborn (46:14): Huh. Interesting. Yeah, I had not considered that. I just would've assumed. I mean, it makes sense, right? If your lifestyle gets uprooted, then you certainly change how you spend your money and where you spend it. Are you seeing those changes reflect in terms of sales or numbers on that side of things, on that side of the business?\n\nChristian Lepley (46:42): You referring to? How have sales been since the pandemic\n\nCole Heilborn (46:45): Shut\n\nChristian Lepley (46:46): Down?\n\nCole Heilborn (46:47): Is your fear is the challenge, are you seeing that manifesting? Are there tangible results that are showing that happening?", "token_estimate": 163, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "46:14", "end_timestamp": "46:47", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "8fca649b-f2a0-4a2f-acb2-02aea3e20526", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Christian Lepley (46:54): Well, we certainly, when everything shut down, our sales did change. They did reduce and everything shrunk. Everything just contracted. So that happened for sure. All of a sudden, especially when you lose expo sales and there's no longer events where we've actually sold product and then our retailers literally closed down. Of course then, like everybody, we all know this story. E-commerce shot through the roof. That was fine, but it did not make up for the sports specialty channel, the brick and mortar channel. It did not make up for that. It did not make up for all the fun we have at the New York City Marathon, selling thousands of gels to people. So all those sales just went away. So our sales did shrink. But then what we're seeing now based upon this is a leveling off, and it's solidifying and then kind of building back. So that's why I have a lot of optimism for Goo in the future because of the brand loyalty and the brand awareness with just even on just our core, that in all this pent up demand and the fact that one of our goals during the pandemic is to just remain, if nothing else, remain relevant. (47:58): Talk to race directors, talk to consumers. Let's do offers. Let's just be empathetic, but just stay in front, get, keep the brand in front of people. They don't have to necessarily buy right now or do what they're, but just keep it in front of people, entertain people, offer up help. Just be communicative with our community. And then when the time comes, it will grow back. Because one thing you might find in other categories where sales have just gone crazy, if it's bikes or helmets or shoes or whatever, I dunno if that's going to be sustainable sales once the pandemic is over, that might be a risk for some brands in some categories. Whereas we have the opposite. We actually have all upside from where we are (48:37): Versus potential. There's nothing in my mind that says we're going to, gore going to slide down. We're only going up from this point. We started down, now we're just on the way up. So for me, as things starting to lighten up with the vaccine coming online and then the world kind of getting used to the new normal and things start opening up and race directors start to feel comfortable in conducting races in a smaller way, it's all upside for good. So I feel like we we're well positioned. So I feel super comfortable and super optimistic. I would be more worried if we were just killing it right now, knowing that that's going to change sometime next year or something's going to change for the negative. That would be a hard thing to plan for, I think. I don't know. So I think as hard as it is that you had to reduce your resources in your company, but you're reduced for the eventual bring back, I think is a comfortable place to be. And so the marketing and sales and other folks at Go, we're protecting that trajectory. Like just remain relevant, keep our leadership position, have conversations, even if it's the tiniest little VR that's a little Turkey trot that's just in some small town and they need some stuff for their virtual grid bags. Yeah, let's get on some stuff. Let's just remain relevant. Don't be forceful. Just be there for the community. They will pay you back.", "token_estimate": 818, "speakers": ["Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "46:54", "end_timestamp": "46:54", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "0d998e4c-6b9d-4a23-b8db-7366c436494e", "episode_id": "18dab386-e61d-49cb-9356-8864ac6a3355", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4853", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Cole Heilborn (49:51): Yeah. Beautiful. Well, we're about out of time for today, but thank you so much for hopping on the show and sharing when this episode comes out. It'll be well in a couple months from now, so I'm excited to see what I think as we all are, we're excited to see what 2021 holds and hopefully it has some positives for us. But yeah, I wish you guys the best asked.\n\nChristian Lepley (50:16): Well, thank you very much. This was a lot of fun. Yeah. We're also looking forward to 21. It's going to be a fun ride, I think. But what's most interesting is we know we are expecting something different, whereas this year we didn't know what was about to happen in 20. So it'll be a fun ride. I think. So, yeah. Thank you for the time. This was great.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:34): Yeah. If folks want to go to follow along, either with you personally or with the brand, where can folks go to connect\n\nChristian Lepley (50:42): At Goo Energy? Instagram is a great place to connect with the brand and me personally at Christian Lepley as well. But really goo at Go Energy is where you can learn about all the things that we're doing and get entertained along the way.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:54): Okay. And find some sports nutrition if you're interested in learning more.\n\nChristian Lepley (50:58): Sure, absolutely. It's there for you.\n\nCole Heilborn (51:01): Awesome. Cool. Well, Christian, thank you again and have a good rest of your day. I hope you get out on a run or a bike ride this evening.\n\nChristian Lepley (51:08): It's on the plan. Thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (51:09): All right.\n\nChristian Lepley (51:10): Good to talk to you. Alright,\n\nCole Heilborn (51:10): Bye. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 472, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Christian Lepley"], "start_timestamp": "49:51", "end_timestamp": "51:10", "podcast_index": 47, "episode_name": "Ep 47: Christian Lepley | An Awakening | GU Energy Labs", "slug": "ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-47-christian-lepley-an-awakening-gu-energy-labs", "guest_name": "Christian Lepley", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing, GU Energy Labs", "categories": [], "short_description": "An awakening that has lead to company-wide transformation", "podcast_length": "44:69"}, {"id": "303d3201-1ff8-419c-b862-531f9533bf29", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Chatham Baker (00:04): We are best when it's seamless and we're totally invisible, right? We want products to feel and look and give people that are using those products that the same emotions and the same functionality and innovation that they would get from any of the products from those brands.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. Today I'm sitting down with Chatham Baker, the vice President of Brands of Signature Product Group. Chatham, how are you doing?\n\nChatham Baker (01:08): I'm doing great, man. It's good to be on the call with you.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:12): Yeah, likewise this week before Thanksgiving, and so I think we're both ready for this week to be over and get to some Turkey day.\n\nChatham Baker (01:20): Yeah, for sure. I don't think I've ever looked forward to Thanksgiving as much as 2020 has led us to so\n\nCole Heilborn (01:31): Well you, you're in Utah, I got to imagine it's pretty snowy and cold there right now.\n\nChatham Baker (01:36): Yeah, it is. Well, we wish there was always more snow, but the lifts are spinning and we're looking forward to getting as much snow as possible. I'm kind of trying to cool my jets, not get too excited for at least another storm cycle, but yeah, it's winter here and that means more fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:00): Are you one of the guys or is your company one of the companies that on a fresh powder day, there's half the office is empty because you guys are all up on the hill?", "token_estimate": 511, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:04", "end_timestamp": "02:00", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "c46070c1-3766-4c9c-917d-149289afc0c3", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Chatham Baker (02:10): Oh yeah. I mean it's powder days at least. Powder days are predictable when people will be out of the office and then the rest of the year, it's hard to say, but everyone, whether it's a hike or mountain bike, we will definitely lunch rides, but lunch rides can turn into all day things here.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:28): I like that you take off for lunch for an hour and then you realize that half the day has passed and you're like, well, I guess we'll just show up tomorrow.\n\nChatham Baker (02:37): Yeah, we got one more lap. Won't hurt us. Yeah, no, I think we're a small outfit and the idea of, hey, there's things to get done and you got to get those things done, but it's not dependent on sitting in a seat for a certain number of hours or clocking at the right time there to make sure we've got our work done and we're also kind of attentive to our partners outside of the building.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:07): Yeah. Well kind of give us some background on the company. What do you guys do?\n\nChatham Baker (03:13): Yeah, sure. Signature Products Group or SPG as it's known in some circles, we're a licensed product group, so what that means, at least from our perspective, and I've been learning it, I'm starting in on my third year here and I had no idea what licensed products were beforehand. My understanding what licensed products were was University, NCA sweatshirts and Disney characters on cups and things like that. What we are is we are an extension, a product extension for bigger brands. So we design, develop, manufacture, distribute product categories for brands that have a customer base that that product is either will fit in with their brand, DNA or they see an opportunity. So we're there to be the experts in those product categories for those other brands.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:20): And who are some of the brands that you work with on a daily basis?", "token_estimate": 473, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:10", "end_timestamp": "04:20", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "52e149a0-d5bc-4da0-8b00-a6adaf4e098e", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Chatham Baker (04:26): Well, we work with about 10 brands. The brand that's probably most notable for this audience is Carhartt. We do a few categories for Carhartt, and so my daily is definitely being in contact with all types of teams over there for each brand. There's marketing teams, product teams, social compliance teams, and I kind of act as the gatekeeper a little bit for making sure that what we're doing and how we're acting is in accordance to what we've told these brands we would do and acting like those brands so that it's very collaborative and we not much do. It's very transparent with those partners as well. So I kind of make sure that product is a representative of those brands and our marketing is representative of those brands and that in just our behavior in general, how we treat customers, consumers, factories, all of that in wrapped up in a gig.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:48): Interesting. So for a brand like Carhartt, where do you guys step in? Because obviously they have their own product development, they have their own marketing, they have their own distribution. How do you fit into that equation?", "token_estimate": 283, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:26", "end_timestamp": "05:48", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "30b2b84a-84c4-404d-81a7-008b0f905f9f", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Chatham Baker (06:00): Humbly, we're really pretty honored to be part of that organization or just any of the brands that they would trust their brands with us. So we step in, it is a long process. This isn't where it differs than a product design agency is that we probably spend a year to two years working with the brands on what the plan is and developing product. And then we spend the next three to five years making that product contracted that way, and hopefully it's longer. So some of our relationships are new and they're year or two into the partnership, and some of 'em are 20 years old, so it's long-term play for us. And so we'll work with whatever their leadership teams on what categories they want to expand into, where we see those categories having opportunity. And then you start working with various teams. Usually it's product first, and then as product starts to take shape, then you start working with the marketing teams and then you start working with the compliance teams to make sure that products are being made in the same sense and way that those brands would make products. So it's really, we're best when it's seamless and we're totally invisible. We want products to feel and look and give people that are using those products, the same emotions and the same functionality and innovation that they would get from any of the products from those brands.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:48): And so I guess just to clarify, how are you connecting with Carhartt? Are you focusing on separate industries and categories or I guess why would someone like Carhartt, why would a brand Carhartt work with SPG", "token_estimate": 405, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:00", "end_timestamp": "07:48", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "222e3793-d5c6-4b62-9cfd-b265218d3b67", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Chatham Baker (08:02): Carhartt for instance? They're very protective of their brand. I mean 130 year old workwear company that really exists to serve their consumer, which they refer to as hardworking people. And so we try to serve those same people in the same places in the same way that that Carhartt does. So we're trying to connect with the retailers, their marketing teams. Right now where we are in 2020, it's all email and Zoom and smoke signals, and so that's where we're trying to be basically another umbrella or another wing of their company. Not so much we're independent, but all our actions are as if we're treating things the same way that brands would.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:08): I see. So what products then have you worked on with them?\n\nChatham Baker (09:14): We are in a few categories with them. We launched with Carhartt dog gear, which actually was pretty fulfilling to work on because people love their dogs and it's good to make durable functional gear for them. We do belts and wallets, and we do aftermarket automotive accessories like seat covers and things like that for them.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:40): Gotcha. So does a brand, again, like Carhartt come to you because you guys have expertise or you have the ability to gain expertise in an industry like the pet business or the automotive business, and then you can kind of bridge that gap between the brand and those industries? Is that the idea?\n\nChatham Baker (09:57): Yeah, exactly. We either have a built-in expertise already, or we have the ability to focus and invest into that category where a brand might not have the bandwidth or the time or the cache to generate their own expertise and their own focus. So we're kind of like a built in resource for them that they can plug into.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:28): So as your job vice president of brands, what does that entail? How long have you been with the company?", "token_estimate": 474, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:02", "end_timestamp": "10:28", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "0c366237-748a-473a-829e-10a222b2d3da", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Chatham Baker (10:36): I'm basically trying to figure that out on a daily basis, Cole. So the simplified version is one, I'm the liaison to the brands and with that, so I'm communicating with the brands, but I'm also working with our product teams on product design. I work with our marketing team on keeping the look and feel and the messaging of the brands consistent. And then I work with our brand design teams on all those touch points like retail, signage, packaging, labels, signs, banner ads, all of that good stuff that goes into a brand and marketing.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:22): Okay, interesting. So are you managing or are you involved with all 10 of the different brands that SPG works with?\n\nChatham Baker (11:30): Yeah, and so part of my job is keeping track of that and making sure that we're treating each brand independently and with the same level of focus and priority that each brand deserves and ask for from us.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:49): Gotcha. Interesting. So yeah, you've got an interesting job. You kind of have to be an expert on all of these brands and have an intimate understanding of who they are and who they aren't. Is that ever challenging? I mean, a lot of folks at this level are just internal working with the brand and that's all they see and that's all they work on. Whereas you're kind of almost more like an agency perspective where you're managing these different accounts and you have to have a deep understanding of all these different accounts.\n\nChatham Baker (12:20): That side's really like being on a branding agency side. Not all brands that when I start working with them do I understand it intimately. And so that's where it's a lot of learning, a lot of research, a lot of listening, figuring out who the brand's current consumers are, and then who with our products, which are extension products, there are new products for the brands, who will that resonate with and why? And what that connection between this new off core product, how that'll link up with the passionate users and lovers of the brand that are already there.", "token_estimate": 519, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:36", "end_timestamp": "12:20", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "4762f9a0-db22-48ff-b3b8-11db8aec5945", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:08): So I know you said the onboarding process can be a year or two, which obviously, I mean that answers my question, but onboarding you guys isn't as simple as sending over a brand guideline book and being like, all right, you're up to speed now let's go. How deep do you go into essentially becoming an extension of these brands?\n\nChatham Baker (13:32): Brand guidelines really do help, and every brand has different levels at which their brand guidelines are developed. Yeah, that's kind of the starting point and it's getting to know everyone that's working on the brand. We tend to think on the consumer level of brands as these kind of amorphous entities that there's not people that are working on them, there's not individuals making decisions and how those individuals are at the brands. And for the most part, they love working for those brands. They love those brands. I know that feeling well, and so understanding their motivations and thinking and direction helps us craft and give it more of a depth to the licensed product side of things.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:25): Okay, gotcha. So it sounds like it's trying to understand the people who are behind the brand and involved with it. That really helps you understand it all, how it all comes together,\n\nChatham Baker (14:38): Especially this day and age, there's brands have a purpose, they've got a mission statement and taking those to heart, but then also understanding what's between the lines there and what all the pieces and parts that go into fulfilling those purposes and those mission statements to get that more of that depth. So because there's the brand side and there's the product side and there's the business side of things. And so those brands have complex work. And so the more we can mimic and mirror that on our side, the better the relationship will be. Because a lot of it's about relationships and being good people that are carrying the torch of these brands and doing it with integrity is pretty key.", "token_estimate": 502, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chatham Baker"], "start_timestamp": "13:08", "end_timestamp": "14:38", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "c025945f-dbd6-425d-9d74-4830d534e019", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:35): So I'm thinking of the brand guidelines that we've worked with. Clients will send them over, we'll take a look and we'll start to dive into it. Oftentimes I feel like a few different tools in there that I find sometimes really helpful. I'm curious if there's anything in those guidelines that something you dive to first. Do you go to the this and the not this page, or do you go to the consumer persona page? What do you find interesting and what's helpful for you to wrap your head around on what we're working with?\n\nChatham Baker (16:12): Yeah, we're kind of dealing with probably multiple guidelines per brand. And so if you're starting with there's brand bible, that helps definitely why a brand does what they do is key. What is their motivation? Why are they putting things out into the world? And then probably right after that is the people they're serving, who are their customers, who are their loyalists and who are they trying to reach? And after that, once you understand the people, then you can dive into what's the product supposed to promise, if there's product guidelines, product design guidelines, what does that mean? Why is it there? And trying to give thought to all the decision making that goes into what they do so that we're making the same type of decisions.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:19): Gotcha. So are you calling up consumers and doing your own research and developing insights and motivations, or are those resources that the brands typically have? You can look at?", "token_estimate": 379, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chatham Baker"], "start_timestamp": "15:35", "end_timestamp": "17:19", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "938445ce-1982-4502-b023-ceefb8f98485", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Chatham Baker (17:34): We try to take advantage of as many resources the brand has, but at the end of the day, it's also really helpful to get an independent look at that. And so yeah, calling up people where we can and try not to let it be too narrow a focus of like, oh, just my buddies, what do they think of this? But yeah, there's different ways of doing it. Some brands, it is part of the learning process too. Some brands it's like let's get greater insight into why people love the brand or love the product. And then some of it's just learning. We work with a brand called Hooey, which is a rodeo, kind of a new generation of Western brand. And for a lot of us here in Salt Lake and Wasatch Front, we are less aware of what really drives that rodeo loving consumer. So it's a lot of it's learning. And so we've got to go to those places. And those people that love rodeo and are passion, they're passionate about it the same way that people are passionate about snowboarding or climbing. And so we've got to pick up and learn what those passions are and those kind of emotional drivers.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:57): Yeah, interesting. So switching gears a little bit. Before I met you, I had no idea that this was an industry or a job that one could do. I just assumed if it had the Carhartt logo on it, it came directly from Carhartt hq. What were you doing prior to this? What's been your career history and journey?\n\nChatham Baker (19:18): Yeah, this is a learning experience for me too. I'm coming from the brand side of things. I was creative director at Smith Optics for a while. I was in-house there for just short of and about 14 years being involved with the brand before in-house. I was a contract graphic designer for them. So I cut my teeth in the wilds of around Ketchum, Idaho, and then about three, almost four years in Portland, Oregon when Smith relocated there.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:55): Okay, gotcha. And why make the move from Smith to SPG?", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:34", "end_timestamp": "19:55", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "adbc05ce-1e87-4b11-acd2-0183c82b3660", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Chatham Baker (20:01): That was a hard move really, but it's a simple kind of explanation is that my wife and I had a little girl and we introspectively looked and said, Hey, where do we want to raise her? And we had lived for a long time in Idaho, in the mountains there in Sun Valley area, and we always missed it. And so the opportunity came up to be able to move to Park City and give her our daughter more of the life that we had envisioned for. And so we did it. So Heartbreakingly in a lot of ways left Smith, but to kind of take on this new adventure. And I didn't totally know anything about licensed product or that world either. I just knew about brands and trying to make product that people loved and got to enjoy. And so there's a lot of parallels there. And so that's what brought us to be somewhat kept secret of Utah.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:16): It's not so kept secret anymore.\n\nChatham Baker (21:19): No, hey, we're here. It's not like we discovered it. Right. And I'll tell you, parking lots at trail heads and at ski resorts will tell you that there's a lot of people that are on the same program. We are.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:37): I feel like, what is it? But I only feel like it's in the last few years. Maybe it's just my own awareness of it, but Utah has become such a hot destination. Maybe that's not true, but I feel like that's what I've seen.", "token_estimate": 342, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:01", "end_timestamp": "21:37", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "feae83f6-f01a-4f1b-898c-85e445d087f3", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Chatham Baker (21:54): That's what it feels like. I don't know, probably someone that's been here longer and has a true local status could shed more light on it. But I mean, Utah's amazing from deserts to mountains and mountain biking and climbing and snowboarding and skiing, whether it's back country resort, I mean it's just got, and it, it's so accessible from Salt Lake City. We think of, certainly live in Portland, Portland's an outdoor town. You don't tell anybody in Portland that they're not passionate about the outdoors. And I think Denver is similar and we think of those as being right in the heart of it, but you still driving, you, you're spending some time in, this is a general statement, I've got lots of great friends in the cities, but Salt Lake, it's just that much easier. And so that ease kind of takes that barrier away from getting access into some great areas and great recreation.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:02): Yeah. So you started out as a graphic designer doing design work. Do I dive a little more into that part of your background? I think I'm curious how you went from doing graphic design to, I mean, I can see the transfer to Smith and being a creative director, but then with the new role with SPG, I feel like those are very different worlds while they have some common skill sets. How did that go about and what were you doing way back", "token_estimate": 342, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:54", "end_timestamp": "23:02", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "750e4783-c0b7-4d4c-a798-7d8bdeb71060", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Chatham Baker (23:30): In the, yeah, we can go way back. It will make this seem even less planned out than it already doesn't seem. I went to school for fine art and I basically chose a school that had a good education. I could snowboard, I could study art, I could play soccer. And so I studied painting and with a really much more prudent minor in creative writing. And so I got towards the end of being done with school and I started to panic a little bit that I had no applicable real world skills that would lend themselves to having a job that actually paid a living wage. And around that time of panic during my senior show, someone came up to me and was like, oh, do you do graphic design? I said, yes, I knew what Photoshop was. They said, oh, well, we're going really like your art, but I'd like you to come down to New York and let's talk. And so I spent the next waking hours learning everything I could about graphic design. And\n\nCole Heilborn (24:52): So you didn't have any idea? Zero. Zero.\n\nChatham Baker (24:56): And I basically, so I moved to New York. I'm a northwest kid, so I was already a fish out of water on the east coast. And then furthermore in New York, I mean it was bad. At one point someone asked me about, oh, how do you feel about Swiss fonts? And I had zero idea what they were talking about. I mean, I bombed interview after interview after kind of deciding that the reason I moved there wasn't the job that I wanted, which was in kind of the magazine world to date myself. Magazine world was a big deal at the time, and I floundered there for a little bit. I was doing freelance graph design. I taught myself enough that I could and had enough of a perspective that I could get some work. And then it started snowing out west and I had friends with a couch that I could crash on in Sun Valley and I'd never been there. And I was like, well, I'll go out for a couple months and snowboard and kind of see how things go.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:00): So how long were you in New York before you decided to leave?", "token_estimate": 510, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:30", "end_timestamp": "26:00", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "06286c73-c4bf-4f29-8c9d-a1e6970cb20a", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Chatham Baker (26:03): Oh, months, I don't know, eight months. Not long enough. And I was like, I'll be back. And that was 20 years ago. So I love to visit, but I kept at graphic design and started doing more freelance and just snowboarded and started doing some things snowboarding and got introduced to somebody at Smith who was Corey Smith, who was their team manager at the time. And he gave me a few t-shirt designs to do, paid me in goggles. And then they gave me some goggle design, goggle graphic designs to do. And then I me in to help launch snow helmets at the time. And so was color graphics. And I knew nothing about, I didn't wear a helmet at the time, and so I had to learn why are people wearing helmets and what would be cool? And so it started this idea of having to learn more about what the projects you were given. (27:06): And so that learning, why are we, well, we've got to learn if we're going to do graphics and colors, well, what do people want? What's the consumers thinking? What's the user end user thinking about this? And so it just continued and why do they want it from Smith? So, okay, well who is Smith to people? And so you have to start thinking not just about graphic design, but the brand side of things. And so that kind of led me to be asking those questions and I was given some opportunity there to grow and just kind of kept growing. And so at one point we started really asking the questions at Smith of like, well, who is Smith and why do we do this? And if you asked anybody in the building, everyone would give you a different answer. And so we realized we had to kind of centralize that. (28:02): We had to create a shared message and a shared mission for just people. We can't consistently talk about Smith internally, how do we expect the people on the outside to have a concrete idea? And so we ended up replatforming the brand, giving it kind of a new meaning, a more sharper focus and perspective on who we were and who we were going to be. And that led to us developing new branding, new ways of thinking about product. And so I just became more wrapped up in that. And so ended up being heavily focused on what the brand side of things, not just the aesthetics and logos and things like that. So", "token_estimate": 563, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker"], "start_timestamp": "26:03", "end_timestamp": "26:03", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "f689e64c-f7e1-4ec0-9c7a-6ab09ab6ca6e", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (29:00): That\n\nChatham Baker (29:00): Fits nicely into what I do now, which is interpreting brands, because you're always interpreting brands, even if you're inside, maybe you spend 20%, 20% of your time inventing what's next, and then figuring out how to interpret that through all the touch points that your brand is out in the marketplace.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:25): So that was a 20 year career summary and a minute 30,\n\nChatham Baker (29:31): I don't know, I probably forget most of it, but no, it's been fun. I mean, Smith was an amazing place to work. It's an amazing group of people and it makes an product and it was definitely an honor to get to be part of that and help drive a lot of that while I was there.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:50): Did you ever figure out what your opinion on a Swiss font was?\n\nChatham Baker (29:54): Yes, but 15 years too late. So now I just got so into that side of things. I think part of that was just personal stubbornness that was like, oh, I don't know that. Well, I will. And just spent, it started as learning and then learning that subject matter then becomes a passion. And you go down the rabbit hole, man, and I haven't come out since, so I don't get to do that. I don't get to push pixels and really be in the weeds as much as I used to, and I definitely miss it, but you got to go with the river sometimes. So\n\nCole Heilborn (30:46): I don't know that, maybe it's just some of the folks that I've chatted with. But on this podcast, a lot of stories have come up kind of similar to what you just described. Someone asks, can you do this? Or What do you think of this? And a lot of the time, early in their career, people just kind of bs their way through whatever it is they're being asked to do. And I think there's like, you need to have a certain level of, oh, I'll just figure this out and kind of fake it till you make it that personality. It can come around and bite you, but also I feel like you have to just dive in without fully knowing everything. I dunno, curious your thoughts on that.", "token_estimate": 513, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chatham Baker"], "start_timestamp": "29:00", "end_timestamp": "30:46", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "b4b74f7d-b6fe-4860-9a3b-9ec8f71a8ea5", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Chatham Baker (31:29): Yeah. Well, I think we'd all be naive to think that we have all the answers at the start of anything. And so you just kind of learn that there's always going to be a certain amount that you don't know. Even if you've been doing something for 50 years, something's always going to go wrong and you don't know everything. And I think the more you come to grips with the idea that it's just part of it that you don't know where at the beginning, certainly when you're just getting out and figuring out what you want to do in the world that what you don't know is maybe feels more intimidating or you're more aware of what you don't know or that there's something you don't know. And so I think that the mentality that embraces what you don't know or the unknown, that mentality helps you. And at a certain point, you just kind of start figuring, factoring that into most things that you do in life. It's having the humility to understand that, yeah, I don't know this and it's not going to be perfect, but hopefully it's good.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:58): Yeah. So in your job now, I mean you're still pretty fresh with SPG, relatively speaking, what are some of the challenges you're working through right now? What are some of the things that you don't know about this current industry or this job that you're trying to figure out?", "token_estimate": 337, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:29", "end_timestamp": "32:58", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "b344fbe7-ebe7-4db3-97fb-da992888f990", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Chatham Baker (33:20): I think the amount of things I don't know now is immeasurable, but one of the big learning curves that I deal with on a daily basis is learning more about how to run a business. My role at Smith is pretty insulated from needing to worry about p and l sheets or margins or logistics. How are we going to be able to supply the demand or how are we going to be able to figure out the demand is that was a whole different wing of the company. Those are other people that were experts working on that. And so I just focused on what I did. But at SPG, it's all so intertwined. It's the curse and the advantage of working at a small privately held company that you've got to in being in a leadership role that you have to be aware of all those things. And so that's been a big learning curve for me or just having to wrap that into the other focuses that needed to bring. (34:44): The other idea too is that they brought me in here as they were recognizing that they needed to find new paths to grow and not just grow, but survive. And so bringing a lot of how to implement process, how to the right process without it getting bureaucratic, how to implement change in an organization where that change is sustainable and embraced by everyone that's being asked to change tough. And so it's a lot of the interpersonal stuff or interpersonal stuff with colleagues and associates and everyone I work with, that's as a part of the job is just figuring out how to make a Carhartt dog collar live up to the high ask of what Carhartt product needs to be.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:46): Yeah. Do you feel like the skills that you've acquired in the creative space, those are translated or somehow loosely translate to more of the management business side of what you're doing now?", "token_estimate": 446, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:20", "end_timestamp": "35:46", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "aefd9d04-5486-43d4-bee0-1077039c5f20", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Chatham Baker (35:59): Yeah. That's something that really spent a lot of time over the last few years trying to get better at, which is if you're in the creative space, you can come up with ideas all day and getting people to buy into those ideas and making sure that the ideas you're coming up with are the right ideas or at least the right enough ideas for an organization to move forward with. You've got to work with a lot of different teams, and you've got to learn how to communicate your idea as well so that you can prove to somebody else that you're thinking about something in the right way. And then that idea is the right idea that we should all hold hands and walk forward with. And so that's a big part of working with people is that people may do what you tell them to do, but that's in the short term over the long term, if they don't buy into it, they're not going to do what you're asking 'em to do for very long, rightfully so. So you've got to make sure that everyone feels like the common goals you have are worth it to everybody that's going to be putting in all types of effort to get it done.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:24): Yeah, crazy. I got to imagine you in New York trying to figure out what a Swiss font was. You would never imagine yourself leading a team of people and managing and understanding and interpreting a brand like Carhartt. Now that's got to be crazy feeling.\n\nChatham Baker (37:42): Yeah. I mean, I had trouble figuring out what my next meal would be that wasn't a hot dog that I could afford and what I was going to do for fun that night, let alone what I was going to be doing down the road. So pretty grateful and appreciative for all of it. But yeah, worlds apart, but I don't know, you just take your day and take it as it comes.", "token_estimate": 443, "speakers": ["Chatham Baker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "35:59", "end_timestamp": "37:42", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "4ecc0e43-621c-484f-818d-ecf0d67d0ecf", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (38:15): Any thoughts or advice for folks who are in the creative industry who maybe have what it takes and have the qualities to be a good leader in the future, but they're still kind of stuck in the mindset of, all I want to do is the creative operations. I have to wonder how many great leaders are out there, don't have really a chance to become that leader because they're so focused on their craft, which nothing wrong with that, but I think there's a great need or there maybe is a need for those creative leaders to make this step into more of that management position like you've done.\n\nChatham Baker (38:53): Yeah, I mean, I think there's no right path, and I think that there's certainly some of the most talented creatives out there that are just doing their craft. And hey, maybe part of it is that I am not as talented as a lot of those people doing that, and so I had to rely on other ways to maintain a job and put past money in my pocket. And I think a lot of it is that nothing, whether you're working with teams or on a team or working independently, it's probably a shared tenant that you always putting the work in and putting the diligence and working hard, that doesn't go away. And you can, if you're working your ass off and trying to learn, that'll make up for a lot of shortcomings of whether your idea didn't get brought in, whether you're facing, oh, older management. It's like, oh, this young kid doesn't know what he's talking about, or My ideas better and just keep working and making sure that it's not all fun, but you got to have enough fun in your life that it makes the parts that suck worth getting through. It's a job, man. I think being able to separate yourself from idealism a little bit just to kind of get through the day is part of surviving so that you can enjoy yourself when you get the opportunity and hopefully work's something you enjoy, right? You should enjoy work. There's no reason that you should have a job that you don't enjoy. And if you do, there's other jobs out there if you can find him and hustle for.", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chatham Baker"], "start_timestamp": "38:15", "end_timestamp": "38:53", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "30082f64-7781-49d9-8cb4-9cfa3f543971", "episode_id": "3cdf1b85-e126-4968-acc5-2c3907b022dd", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4858", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (41:05): Yeah. Cool, man. Well, we're about out of time for today, but I appreciate you taking the time to chat and share, well, a mix of everything. I think it's really cool. I mean, again, I had no idea that your job was a job that people can have, and it's cool to hear a little bit of your backstory and how the struggle as an early graphic designer has turned into a job that you didn't know existed. So thank you.\n\nChatham Baker (41:34): Yeah, absolutely. I hope I didn't get too off track there for you.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:42): No, it was great. If folks want to follow along, either with you or with SPG, where can they go to connect?\n\nChatham Baker (41:51): Yeah, it's pretty limited as far as our website, but you can check it out. It's s spg company.com. It's a little bit of a taste of what we do. Not much we tried it to really be brand forward and what we do being exhibited by the brands. If you were looking for inane chickens and hip hop and pictures of, nothing hit me up at Grandma Chatham.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:25): Perfect. Well, Chatham, thank you again. I appreciate you taking the time and I hope you guys have a great Thanksgiving and hopefully get some good skiing in this winter.\n\nChatham Baker (42:34): Yeah, you too, man.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:35): Alright, take care. Talk you soon. Bye. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 384, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chatham Baker"], "start_timestamp": "41:05", "end_timestamp": "42:35", "podcast_index": 48, "episode_name": "Ep 48: Chatham Baker | Integrated Licensing | SPG Company", "slug": "ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-48-chatham-baker-integrated-licensing-spg-company", "guest_name": "Chatham Baker", "guest_title": "Vice President of Brands, SPG", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your brand is their brand", "podcast_length": "44:70"}, {"id": "b19c94a7-badc-43e5-9a2f-ecf26ae445c8", "episode_id": "de533042-bfb1-460a-881f-10e1267ee3b2", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4855", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Sean Slobodan (00:00:05): If you are real and open and emotionally available to someone that you're getting to know, they're more likely to reciprocate that and be open and available to you. And that's not going to happen with everyone. But I think everyone that ended up in the film, it happened with to a certain degree and I think we were able to kind of trust\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:30): That. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hor. Today I'm sitting down with two filmmaker friend Shandy Cano and Sean s Boden, respectively, producer and director over at Local Studio. Guys, how are you doing\n\nSean Slobodan (00:01:19): Great. Thanks for having us. Doing good.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:22): Yeah, thanks for taking the time to chat today. You guys are well, where are you guys calling in from?\n\nShandi Kano (00:01:29): Salt Lake City-ish.\n\nSean Slobodan (00:01:31): Heart of the Wasatch Mountains,\n\nShandi Kano (00:01:33): Heart of the Wasatch Mountains. We're at my house in Sandy, Utah, right at the mouth of Little Cottonwood Canyon. It's snowing outside. Praise the Lord. It's been like three weeks. We need this.", "token_estimate": 431, "speakers": ["Sean Slobodan", "Cole Heilborn", "Shandi Kano"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:05", "end_timestamp": "00:01:33", "podcast_index": 49, "episode_name": "Ep 49: Sean and Shandi | An Award Winning Story | Forever is Now", "slug": "ep-49", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-49", "guest_name": "Sean Slobodan and Shandi Kano", "guest_title": "Filmakerrs", "categories": [], "short_description": "Filmmakers Sean Sloboda and Shandi Kano join me to discuss their latest, award-winning, documentary film", "podcast_length": "44:71"}, {"id": "92c4fa70-174f-4dae-93fc-1d96b4c16a6b", "episode_id": "de533042-bfb1-460a-881f-10e1267ee3b2", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4855", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:01:44): I'm excited to have you guys on the show for a number of different reasons. Obviously this podcast, we talk a lot about marketing and we interview folks in the marketing world. We've had a few filmmakers on, not as many as I would like. So you guys are kind of, I think second, maybe third interview. We've had some filmmakers on the show, and so I'm excited to chat with you guys. You guys recently wrapped up a pretty awesome project that I'm excited to learn more about and share with our audience. But first, let's do a little background on Local Studio, who you guys are and how you guys started directing and producing.\n\nShandi Kano (00:02:22): You want me to take that? Well, I mean your local studio. I'll fill in what I can.\n\nSean Slobodan (00:02:27): Well, so let's see. I am originally from Vancouver Island Canada up in British Columbia and moved down to Utah in 2006 to study film at Brig Union University and just kind of ended up staying down here. I met my wife at school and Network, just kind of started expanding as I was in school and starting to work freelance. As soon as I got out of school. I also worked in corporate land as well on some marketing teams and learned a lot there. But eventually in 2015, after kind of continuously dabbling in freelance and contract work, decided to go out on my own and kind of made the leap of faith. And that's when local studios started, was in 2015 and definitely been working in the industry long before then. But that was the official jump for local studio and things just kind of started trickling along. I don't think, actually it was pretty much crickets for the first six months I was on my own. But Shandy and I met, let's see,\n\nShandi Kano (00:04:00): September, 2016.\n\nSean Slobodan (00:04:01): So in 2016,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:04:03): That's a very specific date. I feel like something really memorable or terrible happened that day.", "token_estimate": 483, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Shandi Kano", "Sean Slobodan"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:44", "end_timestamp": "00:04:03", "podcast_index": 49, "episode_name": "Ep 49: Sean and Shandi | An Award Winning Story | Forever is Now", "slug": "ep-49", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-49", "guest_name": "Sean Slobodan and Shandi Kano", "guest_title": "Filmakerrs", "categories": [], "short_description": "Filmmakers Sean Sloboda and Shandi Kano join me to discuss their latest, award-winning, documentary film", "podcast_length": "44:71"}]
